900:
** βᾰθύνω**:—
_passive_, _pluperfect_ βεβάθυστο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 39.305}}: (βαθύς):— **deepen, hollow out,** βάθυνε δὲ χῶρον ἅπαντα, of a torrent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.421}}; ἔσκαψε καὶ ἐβάθυνε dug and **dug deep,** {{NT|NT.Luke.6.48}}; ἐβάθυνε πέδον ταρσῷ, of a dog, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 47.239: _metaphorically_, ὁ λιμὸς βαθύνει ἑαυτόν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.12.3}}: especially as military term, **deepen,** τὴν φάλαγγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.23, 8.5.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 25.11}}:—_passive_, **become deep, be deepened,** λίμνη β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.3; κρημνὸς βαθύνεται εἰς ἀπορρῶγα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.21.3}}; νάσως βαθυνομένας ἀπὸ ῥιζᾶν, of Delos, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Hymnus ad Isim” 160; τὸ βαθυνόμενον τῆς ῥηγμῖνος 6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 2.2}}; of a deep wound, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 39.305}}; βαθυνομέναις χερσί **in** or **with the hollowed** hands, {{Refs|[prev. work] 11.180.}}
**2** in Mathematics texts, **add a third dimension,** β. τὰ ἐπίπεδα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus}} **in R.**2.52 K.,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare “in Ti.” 1.146 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:—_passive_, κυκλικῶς βαθυνθέντες {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 59.17.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **sink deep,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.248, 2.402}}; **sink, crumble,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 157.8.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **go deeply into a subject,** βαθύνας θεωρῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.622 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

901:
** βᾰθύς**, βαθεῖα
_Ionic dialect_ βαθέᾰ, βαθύ; _feminine_ βαθύς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 37, 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus 8}}; _genitive_ βαθέος, βαθείας _Ionic dialect_ βαθέης: _dative_ βαθέϊ, βαθείῃ _Ionic dialect_ βαθέῃ: _comparative_ βαθύτερος, _poetry_ βαθίων [ῑ _Attic dialect_, ῐ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.43], _Doric dialect_ βάσσων (which see): _superlative_ βαθύτατος, poetry βάθιστος}}:—**deep** or **high,** _accusative_ to one's position, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; βαθέης ἐξάλλεται αὐλῆς a court **within a high fence,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.142, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.239}}; ἠϊόνος προπάροιθε βαθείης **the deep,** i.e. **wide,** shore, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.92; τάφρος 7.341, +others; κρατήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 563}}; κύλικες {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1200 (Lyric poetry)}}; βαθὺ πτῶμα a fall **from a high rock,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 796}}; πλευρὰ βαθυτάτη (_vulgar form_ βαρυτάτη), of an athlete, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1193}}; of a line of battle, βαθύτεραι φάλαγγες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 11.6, compare “HG” 2.4.34}}; β. τομή, πληγή, a **deep** cut, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.131a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 35.}}
**2** **deep** or **thick** in substance, of a mist, ἠέρα βαθεῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.7, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.144}}; of sand, ἀμάθοιο βαθείης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.587; ἐπὶ θῖνα βαθύν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.32}}; of ploughed land, νειοῖο βαθείης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.353}}; β. γῆ, opposed to to stony ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 637, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.18.1}}; of luxuriant growth, **deep, thick,** of woods, etc., βαθείης τάρφεσιν ὕλης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.555; βαθείης ἐκ ξυλόχοιο 11.415; βαθὺ λήϊον 2.147, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 107; τοῦ ληΐου τὸ.. βαθύτατον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; λειμών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 652; σῖτος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.17; χλόα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1139 (Lyric poetry)}}; χαίτη, τρίχες, πώγων, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.8, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 41.}}
**2.b** **deep,** of colour, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 21.9}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 6.6, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.13, πορφύριον -ύτερον “PLond.” 3.899.4 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** of quality, **strong, violent,** βαθείῃ λαίλαπι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.306.}}
**3.b** generally, **copious, abundant,** β. κλᾶρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.62}}; β. ἀνήρ **a rich** man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.10; β. οἶκος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 113; β. πλοῦτος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.18, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.82b}}; β. χρέος **deep** debt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).8; στεφάνων β. τέρψις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1200 (Lyric poetry); β. κλέος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.53; κίνδυνος [prev. author] “P.” 4.207}}; β. ὕπνος **deep** sleep, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.65, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.170, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 2.3; εἰρήνη [prev. author] “Tox.” 36; σιωπή 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 99, “BC” 4.109 (_superlative_).}}
**4** of the mind, ἄχος ὀξὺ κατὰ φρένα τύψε β. in the **depths** of his soul, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.125}}; but also, **profound,** φρήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.8; φροντίς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 407; μέριμνα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.60; βαθεῖαν ἄλοκα διὰ φρενὸς καρπούμενος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 593; μουσικὴ πρᾶγμ᾽ ἐστὶ β. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 336}}; βαθύτερα ἤθη **more sedate** natures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 930a}} (but, **more recondite,** i.e. **civilized,** manners, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95)}}: of persons, **deep, wise,** β. τῇ φύσει στρατηγός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 27.4; ταῖς ψυχαῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.24.9}}; also, **deep, crafty,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1001; ἦθος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.468.}}
**5** of time, β. ὄρθρος **dim** twilight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 216, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a, etc.}}; β. νύξ **a late hour** in the night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 34; περὶ ἑσπέραν β. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.179e, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.18.3; βαθὺ τῆς ἡλικίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 514}}; β. γῆρας conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.163 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), compare 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.457 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, +others; β. ὥρα ἔτους 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.7.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-έως**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.66}}; **profoundly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.475 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: _superlative_ βαθύτατα, γηρῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.36. (bṇqu/s, compare βένθος.)}}

902:
** βάϊον**, τό,
={βάϊς}, {{NT|NT.John.12.13}}; for _genitive_ _plural_ βαΐων see at {βάϊς}.
**II** **measuring-rod,** β. δικαίῳ ἑξαπήχει {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 37.3 (5th-6th c.AD),compare “BGU” 1094.12 (6th c.AD).}}

903:
**Βαλαάμ**, ὁ, indecl. (Heb. בִּלְעָם), as in LXX (FlJ has ὁ βάλαμος);
**Balaam** (Num.22-24): 2Pe.2:15, Ju 11, Rev.2:14.†
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

905:
** βαλλάντιον**, τό,
**bag, pouch, purse,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus] 178, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 10 (βαλ-), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 707, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.5}}; παῖς ἐκ βαλλαντίου a supposititious child, {{Refs|Telecl [prev. author] 41.}}
**II** **javelin** (as if from βάλλω), a pun in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Dionysius Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.98d}}. (The spelling βαλλ-is better attested than that with βαλ-,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.354 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}; compare βαλλαντιοτομέω, -τόμος.)

906:
** βάλλω**,
_future_ βᾰλῶ (in _Attic dialect_ Prose only in compounds), _Ionic dialect_ βαλέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.403, βαλλήσω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 222, 1491:}}_aorist 2_ ἔβᾰλον, _Ionic dialect_ προ-βάλεσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.331}}; later _aorist 1_ ἔβαλα {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.6.1 (5.18)}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ _infinitive_ βαλέειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.414, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111, +others, but βαλεῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.387, 14.424}}; _optative_ βλείης in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 219}}, _participle_ βλείς {{Refs|[prev. author] 176, as if from ἔβλην (see. συμβάλλω):}}_perfect_ βέβληκα: _pluperfect_ ἐβεβλήκειν, _Epic dialect_ βεβλήκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.661}}:—_middle_, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ βαλλέσκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.74:}}_future_ βᾰλοῦμαι (προ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 201, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.40, etc., _Epic dialect_ βαλεῦμαι (ἀμφι-) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.103:}}_aorist 2_ ἐβᾰλόμην, _Ionic dialect_ _imperative_ βαλεῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.68.γ}} used mostly in compounds:—_passive_, _future_ βληθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.11, (δια-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 863; also βεβλήσομαι [prev. author] “Or.” 271, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.13, (δια-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.2}}; _participle_ δια-βεβλησόμενος [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.13 (_Epic dialect_ _future_ ξυμ-βλήσομαι, see at {συμβάλλω})}}]: _aorist_ ἐβλήθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.84, etc.: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also has an _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _passive_, ἔβλητο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.675, ξύμβλητο 14.39}}; _subjunctive_ βλήεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.472}}; _optative_ βλῇο or βλεῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.288}}; _infinitive_ βλῆσθαι {{Refs|4.115}}; _participle_ βλήμενος {{Refs|15.495:}}_perfect_ βέβλημαι, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ βεβλήαται {{Refs|11.657}} (but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 20), _optative_ δια-βεβλῇσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24:}}_pluperfect_ ἐβεβλήμην (περι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.22, (ἐξ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.17}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ περι-εβεβλέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.25.}} — _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ βεβόλημαι in special sense, see at {βολέω}.
**A** _active_, **throw**:
**I** with _accusative_ of person or thing aimed at, **throw so as to hit, hit** with a missile, frequently opposed to **striking** with a weapon in the hand, βλήμενος ἠὲ τυπείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.495; τὸν βάλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἀφάμαρτε 11.350, compare 4.473, +others}}; so even in ἐγγύθεν ἐλβὼν βεβλήκει.. δουρί {{Refs|5.73; and δουρὶ ὤμων μεσσηγὺς σχεδόθεν βάλε 16.807}}; but later opposed to τοξεύειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.12; ἐκ χειρὸς β. [prev. work] 3.3.15: with _dative_ instrumenti, β. τινὰ δουρί, πέτρῳ, κεραυνῷ, etc., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.518, 20.288, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.128, etc.: βλήμενος ἢ ἰῷ ἢ ἔγχεϊ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.514: with double _accusative_ _person_ et partis, μιν βάλε μηρὸν ὀϊστῷ 11.583: with _accusative_ partis only, 5.19, 657}}; so τὸν δ᾽ Ὀδυσεὺς κατὰ λαιμὸν.. βάλεν ἰῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.15; δουρὶ βαλὼν πρὸς στῆθος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.144: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἕλκος.., τό μιν βάλε Πάνδαρος ἰῷ 5.795}}; also βάλε Τυδεΐδαο κατ᾽ ἀσπίδα **smote upon** it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 281.}}
**I.2** less frequently of things, ἡνίοχον κονίης ῥαθάμιγγες ἔβαλλον {{Refs|23.502}}; of drops of blood,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.536, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1390: _metaphorically_, κηλὶς ἔβαλέ νιν μητροκτόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1200, compare “HF” 1219}}; of the sun, ἀκτῖσιν ἔβαλλεν [θάμνους] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.479; ἔβαλλε.. οὐρανὸν Ἠώς 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.885}} (so _passive_, σελήνη.. δι᾽ εὐτρήτων βαλλομένη θυρίδων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.122 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus))}}; **strike** the senses, of sound, ἵππων ὠκυπόδων ἀμφὶ κτύπος οὔατα βάλλει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.535, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1188, “Ph.” 205 (Lyric poetry)}}; of smell, ὀσμὴ β. τινά {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 412; τάχ᾽ ἂν πέμφιξ σε βροντῆς καὶ δυσοσμίας β. [prev. author] “fragment” 538.}}
**I.3** _metaphorically_, β. τινὰ κακοῖς, φθόνῳ, ψόγῳ, **smite** with reproaches, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1244, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 902, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 895; στεφάνοις β. τινά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.57}} (hence _metaphorically_, **praise,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 2.98); φθόνος βάλλει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 947; φίλημα βάλλει τὴν καρδίαν 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.37.}}
**II** with _accusative_ of the weapon thrown, **cast, hurl,** of missiles, rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βαλὼν βέλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.495; χαλκὸν ἐνὶ στήθεσσι βαλών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5. 346, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.62; ἐν νηυσὶν.. πῦρ β. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.629}}: with _dative_, of the weapon, **throw** or **shoot with** a thing, οἱ δ᾽ ἄρα χερμαδίοισι.. βάλλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12.155; βέλεσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.277: in Prose _absolutely_, β. ἐπί τινα}} **throw** at one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.75; ἐπὶ σκοπόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.29; ἐπίσκοπα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 16; alone, οἱ ψιλοὶ βάλλοντες εἶργον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33: with _genitive_, βάλλοντα τοῦ σκοποῦ}} **hitting** the mark, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sisyphus” 391a.}}
**II.2** generally of anything thrown, εἰς ἅλα λύματ᾽ ἔβαλλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.314; τὰ μὲν ἐν πυρὶ βάλλε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.429; [νῆα] β. ποτὶ πέτρας 12.71}}; εὐνὰς β. **throw out** the anchor-stones, {{Refs|9.137}}; β. σπόρον **cast** the seed, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.26; β. κόπρον “POxy.” 934.9 (3rd c.AD): hence β. ἀρούρας}}**manure,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PFay. 118.21 (2nd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, ὕπνον.. ἐπὶ βλεφάροις β. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.364; β. σκότον ὄμμασι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1535 (Lyric poetry); β. λύπην τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 67.}}
**II.2.b** of persons, β. τινὰ ἐν κονίῃσιν, ἐν δαπέδῳ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.156, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.188; γῆς ἔξω β. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 622; β. τινὰ ἄθαπτον [prev. author] “Aj.” 1333; ἄτιμον [prev. author] “Ph.” 1028}}:—_passive_, ὑπὸ χλαίνῃ βεβλημένος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.164 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); βεβλημένος}}**on a sick-bed,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.Matt.8.14: then _metaphorically_, ἐς κακὸν β. τινά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.221; ὅς με μετ᾽.. ἔριδας καὶ νείκεα β. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.376}}; β. τινὰ ἐς ἔχθραν, ἐς φόβον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 390, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1058}}; also ἐν αἰτίᾳ or αἰτίᾳ β. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 657, “Tr.” 940 (but in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 305 β. αἰτίαν ἔς τινα); κινδύνῳ β. τινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1053.}}
**II.3** **let fall,** ἑτέρωσε κάρη βάλεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.306, compare 23.697; β. ἀπὸ δάκρυ παρειῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.198, compare 114; κατὰ βλεφάρων β. δάκρυα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1206; κατ᾽ ὄσσων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1396; αἵματος πέμφιγα πρὸς πέδῳ β. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 183}}; β. τοὺς ὀδόντας **cast, shed** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 501b2, etc.}}; so βάλλειν alone,{{Refs|[prev. work] 576a4; βοῦς βεβληκώς “SIG” 958.7 (from Ceos).}}
**II.4** of the eyes, ἑτέρωσε βάλ᾽ ὄμματα **cast** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.179; ὄμματα πρὸς γῆν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 582; πρόσωπον εἰς γῆν [prev. author] “Or.” 958: _intransitive_, ὀφθαλμὸς πρὸς τὸ φῶς βαλών}} **aiming at**.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.4.5}}; βαλὼν πρὸς αὐτό **directing one's gaze** at.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.8.10.}}
**II.5** of animals, **push forward** or **in front,** τοὺς σοὺς [ἵππους] πρόσθε βαλών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.572}}; πλήθει πρόσθε βαλόντες (i.e. ἵππους){{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 639; βάλλε κάτωθε τὰ μοσχία 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.44: _metaphorically_, β. ψυχὰν ποτὶ κέρδεα 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 5.12.}}
**II.6** in a looser sense, **put, place,** with or without a notion of haste, τὼ μὲν.. βαλέτην ἐν χερσὶν ἑταίρων {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.574, compare 17.40, 21.104; μῆλα.. ἐν νηΐ β. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.470; ἐπὶ γᾶν ἴχνος ποδὸς β. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 721 (Lyric poetry); φάσγανον ἐπ᾽ αὐχένος β. [prev. author] “Or.” 51; τοὺς δακτύλους εἰς τὰ ὦτα NT.Mark.7.33}}; β. πλίνθους **lay** bricks, {{Refs|“Edict.Diocl.” 7.15}}; **pour,** οἶνον εἰς ἀσκούς [{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.9.17; εἰς πίθον 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.13.12, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.71.5 (variant for{ἐμβ}.)}}]: _metaphorically_, ἐν στήθεσσι μένος βάλε ποιμένι λαῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.513}}; ὅπως.. φιλότητα μετ᾽ ἀμφοτέροισι βάλωμεν **may put** friendship between them,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.16; μαντεύσομαι ὡς ἐνὶ θυμῷ ἀθάνατοι βάλλουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.201; ἐν καρδίᾳ β. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.16}}; but also θυμῷ, ἐς θυμὸν β., **lay** to heart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 706, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 975.}}
**II.6.b** especially of **putting round,** ἀμφ᾽ ὀχέεσσι θοῶς βάλε καμπύλα κύκλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.722}}; of clothes or arms, ἀμφὶ δ᾽ Ἀθήνη ὤμοις.. βάλ᾽ αἰγίδα {{Refs|18.204}}; **put on,** φαιὰ ἱμάτια {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.5.}}
**II.6.c** **place** money **on deposit,** ἀργύριον τοῖς τραπεζίταις {{NT|NT.Matt.25.27.}}
**II.6.d** **pay,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PLond. 3.1177 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1448.5 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.7** of dice, **throw,** τρὶς ἓξ βαλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 968e; ἄλλα βλήματ᾽ ἐν κύβοις βαλεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 330: so probably ψῆφος βαλοῦσα}} _absolutely_, **by its throw,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 751}}: _metaphorically_, εὖ or καλῶς βάλλειν **to be lucky, successful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus lr. p.51 W., “Rh.” 1.10 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** _intransitive_, **fall,** ποταμὸς Μινυήϊος εἰς ἅλα βάλλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.722, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.744, etc.}}; ἄνεμος κατ᾽ αὐτῆς (i.e. νεώς) ἔβαλε {{NT|NT.Act.27.14}}; [ἵππους] περὶ τέρμα βαλούσας **having run** round the post, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.462}}; ἐγὼ δὲ.. τάχ᾽ ἐν πέδῳ βαλῶ (i.e. ἐμαυτήν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1172 (Lyric poetry)}}; λίμνηθεν ὅτ᾽ εἰς ἁλὸς οἶδμα βάλητε **arrive** at.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1579}}; εἴσω β. **enter** a river's mouth, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 8.23}}; βαλὼν κάθευδε **lie down and** sleep, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.20.10}}; τί οὖν, οὐ ῥέγκω βαλών{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.10.29; βαλὼν ἐπὶ τῆς στιβάδος ἐπεχείρει καθεύδειν Anonymus cited in “POxy.” 1368.51; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4.}}
**III.2** in familiar language, βάλλ᾽ ἐς κόρακας **away with you! be hanged!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 835, etc.; βάλλ᾽ ἐς μακαρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 293a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 389.}}
**B** _middle_, **put for oneself,** ὡς ἐνὶ θυμῷ βάλλεαι that thou **may'st lay it** to heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.196, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.218; σὺ δ᾽ ἐνὶ φρεσὶ βάλλεο σῇσιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 107; εἰ μὲν δὴ νόστον γε μετὰ φρεσὶ.. βάλλεαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.435; ἐς θυμὸν βαλέσθαι τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84, etc.}}; εἰς or ἐπὶ νοῦν, εἰς μνήμην, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 24, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.58a, etc. (see. above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6)}}; ἐπ᾽ ἑωυτῶν βαλόμενοι **on their** own **responsibility,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.160, compare 3.71, +others}}; ἑτέρως ἐβάλοντο θεοί, see l. for ἐβόλοντο in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.234; θεοὶ δ᾽ ἑτέρωσε βάλοντο 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.610.}}
**B.2** τόξα or ξίφος ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισιν βάλλεσθαι **throw about one's** shoulder, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.333, 19.372, etc.; ἐπὶ κάρα στέφη β. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1513 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.3** ἐς γαστέρα βάλλεσθαι γόνον **conceive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.28.}}
**B.4** **lay as foundation,** κρηπῖδα βαλέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.3, compare 4.138, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hipp.” 4}}; also, **lay the foundations of, begin to form,** οἰκοδομίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 779b; χάρακα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.105.10, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.161}}; simply, **build,** ἱερὸν περί τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.13}}; β. ἄγκυραν **cast** anchor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.74, etc.; καθάπερ ἐξ ἀγκυρῶν βαλλόμενος ψυχῆς δεσμούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73d.}}
**B.II** rarely, χρόα βάλλεσθαι λουτροῖς **dash oneself** with water, **bathe,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hymnus in Cererem 50 (but λουτρὰ ἐπὶ χροῒ βαλεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 303). (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus -δέλλω in ἐσ-δέλλοντες, ={ἐκ-βάλλοντες}, “IG” 5(2).6.49: ζέλλειν· βάλλειν, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}] Root **g[uglide]el**- 'throw', Sanskrit_galati_ 'trickle', OHG. **quellan** 'spurt up', Lithuanian _gulēti_ 'lie'.)

907:
** βαπτ-ίζω**,
**dip, plunge,** ξίφος εἰς σφαγήν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.18.4; σπάθιον εἰς τὸ ἔμβρυον 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.63}}:—_passive_, of a trephine, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.447; βάπτισον σεαυτὸν εἰς θάλασσαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.166a; β. Διόνυσον πρὸς τὴν θάλασσαν [prev. work] 914d:}} — in _passive_, **to be drowned,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Gnomologium” 47}}; of ships, **sink** or **disable** them, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.51.6, 16.6.2 (_passive_)}}; ἐβάπτισαν τὴν πόλιν **flooded** the city, _metaphorically_, of the crowds who flocked into Jerusalem at the time of the siege, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.3; β. τινὰ εἰσφοραῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.73; β. τινὰ ὕπνῳ “Anthologia Graeca” 11.49 (5th c.BC: Evenus Elegiacus); ὕπνῳ βεβαπτισμένος 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus and 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.3}}:—_passive_, ὡς ἐκ τοῦ βεβαπτίσθαι ἀναπνέουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.63}}; **to be drenched,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 68: _metaphorically_, βεβαπτισμένοι}}**soaked in wine,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176b}}; ὀφλήμασι βεβ. **over head and ears** in debt, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 21}}; γνοὺς βαπτιζόμενον τὸ μειράκιον seeing that he was **getting into deep water,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 277d; β. εἰς ἀναισθησίαν καὶ ὕπνον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 10.9.4; ὁ τῷ θυμῷ βεβαπτισμένος καταδύεται 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 6.19; ψυχή βεβαπτισμένη λύπῃ 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 64.115.}}
**2** **draw wine by dipping** the cup in the bowl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 14.5; φιάλαις β. ἐκ.. κρατήρων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 67.}}
**3** **baptize,** τινά {{NT|NT.Mark.1.4; ἐν ὕδατι εἰς μετάνοιαν NT.Matt.3.11}}:—_passive_, βαπτισθήτω ἕκαστος εἰς ἄφεσιν ἁμαρτιῶν {{NT|NT.Act.2.38; εἰς Χριστόν NT.Rom.6.3, etc.}}:—_middle_, **dip oneself,** {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.5.14}}; **get oneself baptized,** {{NT|NT.Acts.22.16, NT.1Cor.10.2}}:—_passive_, **perform ablutions,** {{NT|NT.Luke.11.38.}}

909:
** βαπτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**dipping in water, immersion,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Mark.7.4, NT.Heb.9.10, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.3.9.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, εἰς κακίας β. οἰχήσεται {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 30.}}
**3** **lethargic sleep,** Archig.and {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.3.}}
**4** **baptism,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.5.2.}}

911:
** βάπτω**,
_future_ βάψω (ἐμ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 959}}: _aorist_ ἔβαψα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 95, etc.}}:— _middle_, _future_ βάψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 51}}: _aorist_ ἐβαψάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 951, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.326 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, _future_ βᾰφήσομαι {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Lev.11.32, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.51}}: _aorist_ ἐβάφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.254 (Myrinus Epigrammaticus), (ἀπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 416}}; in _Attic dialect_ generally ἐβάφην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429e, etc.}}: _perfect_ βέβαμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1176.}}
**I** _transitive_, **dip,** ὡς δ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἀνὴρ χαλκεὺς πέλεκυν.. εἰν ὕδατι ψυχρῷ βάπτῃ (so as to temper the red-hot steel) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.392; β. εἰς ὕδωρ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73e, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.11; τἄρια θερμῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 216}}; εἰς μέλι, εἰς κηρόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 605a29, “de An.” 435a2:}} —_passive_, βαπτόμενος σίδηρος iron **in process of being tempered,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.136a}}; and of coral, **become hard,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.121 (assuming variant).}}
**I.b** of slaughter in Trag, ἐν σφαγαῖσι βάψασα ξίφος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 863; ἔβαψας ἔγχος εὖ πρὸς Ἀργείων στρατ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 95; φάσγανον εἴσω σαρκὸς ἔβαψεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1578 (Lyric poetry)}}; in later Prose, εἰς τὰ πλευρὰ β. τὴν αἰχμήν {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.15; β. τὸν δάκτυλον ἀπὸ τοῦ αἵματος LXX.Lev.4.17.}}
**I.c** also, **dip in poison,** ἔβαψεν ἰούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 574; χιτῶνα τόνδ᾽ ἔβαψα [prev. work] 580.}}
**I.2** **dye,** ἔβαψεν.. ξίφος the sword **dyed** [the robe] **red,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1011}}; β. τὰ κάλλη **dye** the beautiful cloths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 333; β. ἔρια ὥστ᾽ εἶναι ἁλουργά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429d; εἵματα βεβαμμένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.67; τρίχας βάπτειν “Anthologia Graeca” 11.68 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}: _absolutely_ in _middle_, **dye the hair,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 363.4, 4th c.BC(?): Nicolaus Comicus 1.33}}; **glaze** earthen vessels, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.480e}}; of **gilding** and **silvering,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista Alch. p.46 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus: Comedy texts, βάπτειν τινὰ βάμμα Σαρδιανικόν}} **dye** one in the [red] dye of Sardes, i. e. give him a bloody coxcomb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 112}}; but βέβαπται β. Κυζικηνικόν **he has been dyed in** the dye of Cyzicus, i. e. is an arrant coward, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pax” 1176 (see. Scholia).}}
**I.3** **draw** water **by dipping** a vessel, ἀνθ᾽ ὕδατος τᾷ κάλπιδι κηρία βάψαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.127}}; ἀρύταιναν.. ἐκ μέσου βάψασα τοῦ λέβητος ζέοντος ὕδατος **draw** water **by dipping** the bucket, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 25, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 9.8}}; βάψασα ποντίας ἁλός (i.e. τὸ τεῦχος) **having dipped** it **so as to draw** water from the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 610.}}
**I.4** **baptize,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.20 (_passive_).}}
**II** _intransitive_, ναῦς ἔβαψεν the ship **dipped, sank,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 707}}; β. εἰς ψυχρὸν [αἱ ἐγχέλυς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592a18}}; εἰ δ᾽ ὁ μὲν (i.e. ἠέλιος) ἀνέφελος βάπτοι ῥόου ἑσπερίοιο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 858 (ῥόον Scholia): with _accusative_, νῆα.. βάπτουσαν ἤδη κῦμα κυρτόν}} **dipping into**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 71.2}}:—also _middle_, ποταμοῖο ἐβάψατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 951.}}
**II.2** βάψας (i.e. τὴν κώπην) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 225}}. (Cf. O Norse **kuefia** 'dip'.)

915:
** βάρβᾰρος**, ον,
**barbarous,** i.e. **non-Greek, foreign,** not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (but compare βαρβαρόφωνος); β. ψυχαί 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 107}}; especially as _substantive_ βάρβαροι, οἱ, originally **all non-Greek-speaking peoples,** then specially of the Medes and Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 255, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.58, etc.: generally, opposed to Ἕλληνες, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262d, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b5, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28; βαρβάρων Ἕλληνας ἄρχειν εἰκός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1400; β. καὶ δοῦλον ταὐτὸ φύσει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b9; οἱ β. δουλικώτεροι τὰ ἤθη φύσει τῶν Ἑλλήνων [prev. work] 1285a20}}; β. πόλεμον war **with the barbarians,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36 codices}}; ἡ βάρβαρος (i.e. γῆ), opposed to αἱ Ἑλληνίδες πόλεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 187, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.16.}} _adverb_ -ρως, opposed to Ἑλληνικῶς,{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.3.}}
**2** especially of language, φωνὴ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1051, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 341c; γλῶσσα β. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1263, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.57, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus [prev. cited] above, etc.}}; συλλραφαί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Hippias 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; of birds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 199.}} _adverb_, βαρβάρως, ὠνόμασται have **foreign** names, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.3.17.}}
**3** Grammars, of **bad Greek,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 5.20.5}}; τὸ β., of style, opposed to Ἑλληνικόν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.64.}}
**II** after the Persian war, **brutal, rude,** ἀμαθὴς καὶ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 492; τὸ τῆς φύσεως β. καὶ θεοῖς ἐχθρόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.150; σκαιὸς καὶ β. τὸν τρόπον [prev. author] 26.17; β. ἀνηλεής τε 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 477}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}} Eph.2.4: _superlative_, πάντων βαρβαρώτατος θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1573, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.34.}}
**III** used by Jews of **Greeks,** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.2.21.}}
**IV** name for various **plasters,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus and Herasap. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.555. (Onomatopoeic _accusative_ to 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28.)}}

916:
** βαρέω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **βορ-**, see below, _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 10.4}}: _perfect_ βεβάρηκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.17}}:—_passive_, see below:—**weigh down, depress,** βαρήσει ταῦτα τὸ πορθμεῖον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista [prev. cited] (censured, [prev. author] “Sol.” 7); τὴν τῆς δίκης ῥοπὴν β. 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 14; ὅταν τὰ πράγματα βαρῇ τοὺς ἀντιδίκους 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 2.7; ἵνα μὴ τὴν πόλιν βαρῶμεν “IG” 14.830.15 (from Puteoli), compare “POxy.” 1159.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὸ ἔθνος ἐβάρει ταῖς εἰσφοραῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.14.1, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus}} [same place]:—_passive_, κῆρ.. βόρηται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.17; β. διά τινα 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen.64, compare “POxy.” 525.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; β. τῷ ἐκφορίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 6.7 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _accusative_, **to be indignant at,** αὐτῶν τὴν εὐγένειαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.8.1; οὓς βαροῦνται 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.44.}}
**2** **charge** an account, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 126.8 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_ βεβαρηώς **weighed down, heavy,** οῐνῳ βεβαρηότες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.139, compare 19.122}}:—later, _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ βεβαρημένος, μεθυσθεὶς τοῦ νέκταρος β. ηὗδεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b; οἴνῳ β. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.373}}; τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς β. ὑπ᾽ οἴνου{{NT+3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 377; ὠδίνεσσιν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.61; ὕπνῳ “Anthologia Graeca” 7.290 (Stat. Flacc.), “Geoponica” 13.1.8, NT.Luke.9.32 (without ὕπνῳ NT.Matt.26.43); β. τὰ πρόσωπα πένθει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 34; τὰ σώματα πλησμονῇ β. [prev. author] “Mar.” 19; γυνὴ πολλοῖς ἔτεσι β. “PTeb.” 327.25 (2nd c.AD)}}; οἷον βεβαρημένος as though **pregnant,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.8}}:—_passive_, _present_ βαρέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.49}}: _aorist_ ἐβαρήθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 9.8}}: _perfect_ βεβάρηται {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.12.2.}}

917:
Related to: ** βαρέω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **βορ-**, see below, _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 10.4}}: _perfect_ βεβάρηκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.17}}:—_passive_, see below:—**weigh down, depress,** βαρήσει ταῦτα τὸ πορθμεῖον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista [prev. cited] (censured, [prev. author] “Sol.” 7); τὴν τῆς δίκης ῥοπὴν β. 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 14; ὅταν τὰ πράγματα βαρῇ τοὺς ἀντιδίκους 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 2.7; ἵνα μὴ τὴν πόλιν βαρῶμεν “IG” 14.830.15 (from Puteoli), compare “POxy.” 1159.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὸ ἔθνος ἐβάρει ταῖς εἰσφοραῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.14.1, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus}} [same place]:—_passive_, κῆρ.. βόρηται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.17; β. διά τινα 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen.64, compare “POxy.” 525.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; β. τῷ ἐκφορίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 6.7 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _accusative_, **to be indignant at,** αὐτῶν τὴν εὐγένειαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.8.1; οὓς βαροῦνται 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.44.}}
**2** **charge** an account, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 126.8 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_ βεβαρηώς **weighed down, heavy,** οῐνῳ βεβαρηότες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.139, compare 19.122}}:—later, _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ βεβαρημένος, μεθυσθεὶς τοῦ νέκταρος β. ηὗδεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b; οἴνῳ β. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.373}}; τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς β. ὑπ᾽ οἴνου{{NT+3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 377; ὠδίνεσσιν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.61; ὕπνῳ “Anthologia Graeca” 7.290 (Stat. Flacc.), “Geoponica” 13.1.8, NT.Luke.9.32 (without ὕπνῳ NT.Matt.26.43); β. τὰ πρόσωπα πένθει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 34; τὰ σώματα πλησμονῇ β. [prev. author] “Mar.” 19; γυνὴ πολλοῖς ἔτεσι β. “PTeb.” 327.25 (2nd c.AD)}}; οἷον βεβαρημένος as though **pregnant,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.8}}:—_passive_, _present_ βαρέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.49}}: _aorist_ ἐβαρήθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 9.8}}: _perfect_ βεβάρηται {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.12.2.}}

922:
** βάρος** [ᾰ], ους,
_Ionic dialect_ εος, τό, **weight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, etc.}}
**II** **a weight, burden, load,** τέκνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1000, etc.; β. περισσὸν γῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 945: _plural_, βάρη}}**weights,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 850a30.}}
**III** **oppressiveness,** τὸ τῆς ὀσμῆς ἀφόρητον β. {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.9.10}}; βάρος φέρειν to give **trouble,** τινί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1062.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** **heaviness, torpor,** β. ναρκῶδες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.345b; σπληνὸς βάρεα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}; βάρη καὶ δυσαρεστήματα perhaps **feeling of oppression,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.37.15.}}
**V** _metaphorically_, **heavy weight,** σιγῆς β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1256}}; βάρος πημονῆς, συμφορᾶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 939, “Tr.” 325; χρὴ τοῦ βάρους μεταδιδόναι τοῖς φίλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.1}}; ὥσπερ βάρους μεταλαμβάνειν Arist {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“EN” 1171a31; τὰ β. ὅσα ψυχὴν καθέλκοι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.674}}: hence alone, **grief, misery,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 946 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 409; κεφαλῆς πόνος καὶ β. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603b8; τὸ β. ἔχειν [prev. author] “EN” 1126a23; ἐν συνοχαῖς καὶ βάρεσι 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 292.6}}; of **oppressive** demands, β. τῶν ἐπιταγμάτων, τῶν φόρων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.31.5, 1.67.1; τῆς λειτουργίας “BGU” 159.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; οὐκέτι δυνάμεθα φέρειν τὰ β. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.67 (Thrace, 3rd c.AD)}}; κουφίσαι τὰ β. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 7.13 (2nd c.AD).}}
**VI** in good sense, **abundance,** πλούτου, ὄλβου, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1287, “IT” 416; αἰώνιον β. δόξης NT.2Cor.4.17}}; **strength,** στρατοπέδων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.16.4}}; β. τῆς ὑλακῆς **violence** of.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.18.}}
**VII** **weight, influence,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.32.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.70, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 37, etc.}}; **gravity, dignity** of character, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.522e}}; opposed to χάρις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Demetr.” 2.}}
**VIII** Grammars, **stress** of accent, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 98.1.}}
**IX** in Music, ={βαρύτης}, **low pitch,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.11.}}

925:
** βαρύν-ω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247b}}: _imperfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.321}}: _future_ **-ῠνῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 9}}: _aorist_ ἐβάρῡνα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.127c, etc.}}:—_passive_, _present_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.165, etc.}}: _future_ βαρυνθήσομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 697, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.94.9, LXX.Sir.3.26}}: _aorist_ ἐβαρύνθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.480, etc.}}: _perfect_ βεβάρυμμαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Phgn.” (see. below), LXX.Nah.2.9 (10)}}: (βαρύς):—**weigh down, oppress, depress,** εἵματα γάρ ῥ᾽ ἐβάρυνε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.321; βάρυνε δέ μιν δόρυ μακρὸν ἑλκόμενον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.664, etc.}}; ἤν σε βαρύνῃ δίψος epigram cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Sotion Paradoxographus p.39 W.:}} —_passive_, λάθρῃ γυῖα βαρύνεται **he is heavy,** i. e. **weary,** in limb, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.165}}; χεῖρα βαρυνθείς **disabled** in hand, {{Refs|20.480}}; βεβαρύνθαι **to be oppressed** by surfeit, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 810b22, compare “HA” 582b8, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.38; ὑπὸ κόπου 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.39; βαρύνεσθαι τὴν γαστέρα}}**to be pregnant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5; τόκοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1228}}; β. alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 26.270; βαρύνεταί τινι τὸ σκέλος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 220, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117e}}; ὄμμα β., of one dying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 385; βαρυνόμεν᾽ ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα ἐκ παθέων Timo 9}}; also ὃ αὐταῖς ἐβαρύνθη **weighed upon** them, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.15.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **oppress, weary,** ἀστῶν ἀκοὰ κρύφιον θυμὸν β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.84; τοὺς δικαστάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 9}}; of cold, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 456b26}}; **make more grievous,** ἀνίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.425}}:— _passive_, **to be oppressed, distressed,** Simon,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|184.5, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.43, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 820, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1}}; πήμασιν **by** calamities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 836, compare 189 (Lyric poetry); χόλῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 41; κακῇ ὀσμῇ [prev. author] “Ph.” 890; ὀσμῆς [prev. author] “fragment” 697; ξυντυχίᾳ 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 166; διά τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7}}; ὑπό τινων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Nic.Dam. p.38 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἐβαρύνθη ἡ καρδία}}**was made stubborn, was hardened,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Exo.8.15(11), +others; also with _accusative_, τὰ λυπηρὰ τῆς τύχης 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.14; γῆρας 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.32.2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 31, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 43.6; τινά [prev. author] 40.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 32, “POxy.” 298.26 (1st c.AD)}}; τὸν πλοῦτον **to be overloaded with,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.248D.}}
**II** **mark with the grave accent,**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Hdn.Gr. 1.18, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 120.4, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.52f.— βαρύνω 1 is replaced by βαρέω in later Greek.}}

926:
** βᾰρύς**, εῖα, ύ,
_poetry_ _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ βαρεῶν uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 932 (_anapaest meter_): _comparative_ βαρύτερος, _superlative_ βαρύτατος}}:— **heavy in weight,** β. ἀείρεσθαι, opposed to κοῦφος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 310b25, etc.: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly with collateral notion of **strength and force,** χεῖρα βαρεῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.219, compare 89; ἀκμᾷ βαρύς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).51; β. τὸ σῶμα 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μακεδονική” 14}}; of athletes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 31}}; ὀφρύς **bushy,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 48}}; but also, **heavy with age, infirmity** or **suffering,** νόσῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 235; σὺν γήρᾳ [prev. author] “OT” 17; ἐν γήρᾳ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1017; ὑπὸ γήρως 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 9.1; ὑπὸ τῆς μέθης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.596a}}; **pregnant,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PGoodsp.Cair. 15.15 (4th c.AD)}}; β. βάσις **heavy, slow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 966; τυπάδι βαρείᾳ [prev. author] “fragment” 844}}. _adverb_ κοῦφον βαρέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 189d.}}
**2** **heavy to bear, grievous,** ἄτη, ἔρις, κακότης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.111, 20.55, 10.72; Κλῶθες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.197; κῆρες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.548; β. κὴρ τὸ μὴ πιθέσθαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 206 (Lyric poetry)}}; βαρὺ or βαρέα στενάχειν sob **heavily,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.95, 534, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.334, etc.}}: in Trag. and Prose, **burdensome, grievous, oppressive,** β. ξυμφορά, τύχαι, καταλλαγαί, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1044 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 332 (Lyric poetry), 767 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἡδονή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1204; ἀγγελία β. ἢν ἐν τοῖς βαρύτατ᾽ ἂν ἐνέγκαιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c; πόλεμος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.241; βαρὺ κοὐχὶ δίκαιον [prev. author] 21.66; νόσος}}**causing disgust,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1330; αὐδά, ἠχώ, [prev. work] 208 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 791}}; **unwholesome,** χωρίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.12; πλησμονή [prev. author] “Cyn.” 7.4}}; **indigestible,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.115e; β. νότος 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.17.11.}} _adverb_ -έως, φέρειν τι take a thing **ill,** suffer it **impatiently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.19; β. φέρειν ἐπί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.1.1 (but β. φέρειν}} bear **with dignity,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 26.3)}}; β. ἔχειν, with part, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1424b5; πρός τι [prev. author] “Pol.” 1311b9}}; τοῖς λογίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Arg. Euripides Tragicus “Heracl.”}}: _comparative_ βαρυτέρως τινὶ ἐναντιωθῆναι {{LXX|LXX.3Mac.3.1}}; βαρέως ἀκούειν hear **with disgust,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.9.}}
**3** **violent,** ὀργή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 368; μῆνις [prev. author] “OC” 1328; ἀπέχθειαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23a (_superlative_)}}; θυμός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.96.}}
**4** **weighty, grave,** ἐπιστολαί {{NT|NT.2Cor.10.10; αἰτιώματα NT.Act.25.7; τὰ βαρύτερα τοῦ νόμου NT.Matt.23.23}}; **ample,** βαρυτάτην εὐδαιμονίαν τοῖς ἀρχομένοις παρέξειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.14.3.}}
**II** of persons, **severe, stern,** β. ἐπιτιμητής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 77; εὔθυνος [prev. author] “Pers.” 828, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 546; Κύπρι βαρεῖα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.100}}; **wearisome, troublesome,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 894, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 210c, etc.; ξύνοικος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 415, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 753; γείτονες 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.10.6.}}
**II.2** **overbearing,** σεμνότεροι ἢ βαρύτεροι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1391a27 (but σεμνὸς καὶ β. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.42); ὑπερήφανοι καὶ β. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.279c}}; **important, powerful,** πόλις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.5, etc.}}
**II.3** of soldiers, **heavy-armed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.37 (assuming variant)}}; of the ὁπλίτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 833b (_comparative_)}}; τὰ β. τῶν ὅπλων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.76.3.}}
**II.4** **difficult,** ὅρκος γὰρ οὐδεὶς ἀνδρὶ φηλήτῃ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 933.}}
**III** of impressions on the senses,
**III.1** of sound, **strong, deep, bass,** opposed to to ὀξύς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.257, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 208, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1125a14, etc.; βαρὺ ἀμβόασον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 572 (Lyric poetry); φθέγγονται βαρύτατον ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 15; βαρύτατα ὑπακούειν, of diseases, [prev. author] “Prorrh.” 2.39; πενθεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.1}}; especially of musical pitch, **low,** opposed to ὀξύς, βαρυτάτη χορδή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e}}; ἆχος, φωνά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus I, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1235a28, 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.3}} M.; of accent, **grave,** ἀντὶ ὀξείας τῆς μέσης συλλαβῆς βαρεῖαν ἐφθεγξάμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399b; ὀξείᾳ καὶ βαρείᾳ καὶ μέσῃ φωνῇ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1403b30, etc.: hence ἡ βαρεῖα (i.e. προσῳδία)}} **accentus gravis,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.1, etc.; β. τάσις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 307.13; β. τόνος 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 674.13, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 36.5; β. συλλαβή}}**unaccented,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 100.8, +others}} _adverb_ βαρέως **with the accent thrown back,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Pron.” 51.1, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.53b: _comparative_ -ύτερον, opposed to ὀξύτερον}} (ου) opposed to οὗ), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 178a3}} (but, **on a lower note,** αὐλεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 788a22).}}
**III.2** of smell, **strong, offensive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119.}}
**III.3** _adverb_ βαρέως **slowly,** ἐπισπᾶσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 26.6}}. (**guṛr-u-** from **gu[rmacr]ṛ-u-,** Sanskrit _gurús_ 'heavy', Latin _gravis_ (from _feminine_ **gur[schwa]wī-**), Gothic _kaúrus_ 'heavy'.)

927:
** βᾰρῠ-τῑμος**, ον,
**punishing severely,** of the gods below, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 24.}}
**II** **very costly,** {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.13, NT.Matt.26.7.}}
**III** **selling dearly,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.30 (assuming variant).}}

928:
** βᾰσᾰν-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ῐῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 802, 1121, “Ec.” 748:}}_aorist_ ἐβασάνισα, _subjunctive_ βασανίσω variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 618 codex R:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐβασανίσθην: _perfect_ βεβασάνισμαι:—**rub upon the touch-stone** (βάσανος), χρυσόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486d}}: hence, **put to the test, prove,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 747a3 (_passive_), etc.}}; **investigate scientifically,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 3}} of the instances used in inductive inference, ἀπὸ τῶν πανταχόθεν βεβασανισμένων [μεταβαίνομεν] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29.}}
**II** of persons, **examine closely, cross-question,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116, 2.151, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 110, “Ra.” 802, etc.}}; βεβασανισμένος εἰς δικαιοσύνην **having** his love of justice **put to the test,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c, compare 413e, “Smp.” 184a}}; ὑπὸ δακρύων βασανίζεσθαι, i.e. **to be convicted** of being painted by tears (washing off the cosmetic), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.8.}}
**II.2** **question by applying torture, torture, rack** (see. βάσανοςIII), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 616, 618; [δούλους] πάντας παραδίδωμι βασανίσαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8, compare 5.36}}:—_passive_, **to be put to the torture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.14, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1443b31; αἰωνίοις ἀμοιβαῖς βασανισθησόμενοι πρὸς τῶν θεῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.19}}; **to be tortured** by disease (censured by {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 6), NT.Matt.8.6; ὑπὸ τῶν κυμάτων [prev. work] 14.24}}; of animals, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.38}}: _metaphorically_ of the earth,{{Refs|[prev. work] 6.10.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_ of style, **strain,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 10.6; βεβασανισμένος}}**forced, unnatural,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 55.}}

930:
** βᾰσᾰν-ιστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**examiner, questioner, torturer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.40, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.498d}}; **gaoler, **NT.Matt.18.34.
**II** **one who tests,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.247c}}:—_feminine_ **βᾰσᾰν-ίστρια**, **examiner,** ἐπῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 826.}}

931:
** βᾰσᾰν-ος**, ἡ,
**touchstone,** on which pure gold leaves a yellow streak, ἐς βάσανον δ᾽ ἐλθὼν παρατρίβομαι ὥστε μολύβδῳ χρυσός {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 417; χρυσὸν τριβόμενον βασάνῳ [prev. author] 450, compare 1105; παρατρίβεσθαι πρὸς τὰς β. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 793b1, compare “HA” 597b2: _metaphorically_, β. τοῦ ἀρώματος (i.e. τοῦ κινναμώμου) τὴν αἶγα εἶναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.4.}}
**II** **the use of this as a test,** χρυσὸς ἐν β. πρέπει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.67}}: generally, **test, trial of genuineness,** οὐκ ἔστιν μείζων β. χρόνου [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus] 175.1; δόμεν τι βασάνῳ ἐς ἔλεγχον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.20; σοφὸς ὤφθη βασάνῳ θ᾽ ἁδύπολις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 510 (Lyric poetry), compare 494; βάσανον λαμβάνειν περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 648b}}; εἰς β. εἶ χερῶν wilt come to **a trial** of strength, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 835; πλοῦτος β. ἀνθρώπου τρόπων 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 232.5}}; [νόσου] ἔσχ᾽ ἐπὶ σοὶ βάσανον had **experienced** it in you, i. e. you had had it first, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1320}}; βάσανον ὑποκείσονται will be subjected to **a test,** of candidates, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 58.25 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** **inquiry by torture,** ἐς πᾶσαν β. ἀπικνέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.110; εἰς β. αἰτεῖν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.88; ἐξετάσαι διὰ βασάνων “SIG” 780.12 (Astypalaea, Aug.)}}; especially at Athens, used to extort evidence from slaves, εἰς β. δέχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.12; εἰς β. παραδοῦναι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.17; ἐκ βασάνων εἰπεῖν [prev. work] 12}}: in _plural_, **confession upon torture,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.24, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1355b37.}}
**III.2** **agony** of battle, ἡ κατὰ τὸ ἔργον β. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.24}}; **tortures** of disease, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.4.24; compare ἐπάγρυπνος β. 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 211.28; also ψυχικαί [prev. author] 182.19}}; **torments** of hell,{{NT|NT.Luke.16.23.}}
**III.3** **trespass-offering,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.6.17.—Oriental word.}}

932:
** βᾰσιλ-είᾱ**,
_Ionic dialect_ **βᾰσιλ-ηΐη**, ἡ, **kingdom, dominion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, etc.; παιδὸς ἡ β. 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 52}}; **hereditary monarchy,** opposed to τυραννίς, ἐπὶ ῥητοῖς γέρασι πατρικαὶ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13; βασιλείας εἴδη τέτταρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b20}}; ἡ πρώτη πολιτεία μετὰ τὰς β. after **the age of monarchies,** {{NT|[prev. work] 1297b17: _metaphorically_, ἐποίησεν ἡμᾶς β. NT.Rev.1.6; β. τῶν οὐρανῶν NT.Matt.3.2; τοῦ θεοῦ [prev. work] 6.33, etc.}}
**2** **kingly office,** β. καὶ στρατηγία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273a37.}}
**3** at Athens, **the office of the archon** βασιλεύς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.3.1.}}
**4** _passive_, **being ruled by a king,** τῆς ὑπ᾽ ἐκείνου βασιλείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.43.}}
**II** **diadem,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.47, “OGI” 90.43 (from Rosetta).}}
**III** **reign,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]331.40 (Pergam.), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.1, “POxy.” 1257.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; so αἱ β. **the reigns of the Kings,** title of book of “VT”; **accession to the throne**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 646.12 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** concrete, **His Majesty,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.1, LXX.1Mac.6.47.}}

933:
** βᾰσίλ-ειον**,
_Ionic dialect_ **βᾰσιλ-ήϊον**, τό, **kingly dwelling, palace,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.3, etc.}}; more common in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 178, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352a11, etc.}}
**b** **seat of empire, capital,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.15.3, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.18, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.25.}}
**2** **royal treasury,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.31.}}
**II** **tiara, diadem,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.1.10}}, Roussel **Cultes Égyptiens** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|233 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), “OGI” 90.45 (from Rosetta), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.358d, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 3.11, 4th c.AD(?): Horapollo 1.15: _metaphorically_, τὸ β. τῆς εὐπρεπείας}} **diadem** of beauty, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.5.16.}}
**III** ={ἅλιμος}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.91}}; ={λευκόϊον}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.123.}}
**IV** Βασίλεια, τά, **festival of Zeus Basileus,** in Boeotia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 7.552, Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.153, “IG” 12(1).78; at Olbia, “IPE” 1.105.}}

934:
** βᾰσίλ-ειος**, ον,
also α, ον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 589, “IG” 12.115}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ **βᾰσιλ-ήϊος**, η, ον, also **βᾰσιλ-ῇος** Melinnoap.{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.7.12, “Hymnus ad Isim” 138}}:— **royal,** δεινὸν δὲ γένος βασιλήϊόν ἐστι κτείνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.401; ὁ β. θρόνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, etc.}}; used by Trag. in Lyric poetry, β. οἶκοι, μέλαθρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 157, “Ch.” 343}}; ἰσχύς, τιάρα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 589, 661}}; νόστος ὁ β. **the king's** return,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8; τοῖς β. νόμοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 382}}; compare πῆχυς.
**2** **of the archon** βασιλεύς, ἡ β. στοά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.115, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.1, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.3.1}} (also of the **basilica** of Herod at Jerusalem, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.11.5).}}
**3** 'royal', i.e.**choice,** μύρον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.19, 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 2}}; compare βασίλεια· γένος ἰσχάδων, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**4** Ἄρτεμις βασιληΐη, divinity in Thrace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33.}}

935:
** βᾰσῐλ-εύς**, ὁ,
_genitive_ έως, _Epic dialect_ ῆος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ῆϝος “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 104,135H.: _accusative_ βασιλέα, _contraction_ -ῆ}} Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 781.24 (Lyric poetry): _nominative_ _plural_ βασιλεῖς}} _Aeolic dialect_ **-ηες**{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 6.4, “IG” 12(2).6 (Mytil.), -ειες [prev. work] 646a45, +others, _Epic dialect_ -ῆες}} old _Attic dialect_ **-ῆς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 188, 960 (both Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 1.430: _accusative_ _plural_ βασιλέας “IG” 12.115, later βασιλεῖς [prev. work] 2.243, etc.}}:— **king, chief,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: frequently with collateral sense of **captain** or **judge,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 202; διοτρεφέες β. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.445, etc.; θεῖοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.691, etc.}}; later, **hereditary king,** opposed to τύραννος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160b3, etc.}}; but also of tyrants, as Hiero, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.23}}; of Gelo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161}}; of Pisistratus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 123, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 61}}: joined with a _substantive_, βασιλεὺς ἀνήρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.170, etc.; ἀνὴρ β. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90}}; ἄναξ β. lord **king,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 5, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.1: with _genitive_, β. νεῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 114 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οἰωνῶν β., of the eagle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.21}}: _comparative_ βασιλεύτερος **more kingly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.160, 392, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.533, 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.7}}: _superlative_ βασιλεύτατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.69.}}
**b** of the gods, Ζεὺς θεῶν β. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 886, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 128.2, etc. (in this sense 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses ἄναξ)}}; as cult title of Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.90 (Lebad.), “SIG” 1014.110 (from Erythrae), etc. (but Ζεὺς β., = Ahuramazda, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.21, +others, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 4.20.3)}}; ὁ μέγας β., of **God,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Psa.47(48).2, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.107}}: _superlative_ βασιλεύτατοι τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 29.5.}}
**2** as a title of rank, **prince,** β. εἰσὶ καὶ ἄλλοι πολλοὶ ἐν.. Ἰθάκῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.394, compare 8.390}}, etc.; of Cyrus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.16.}}
**2.b** **descendant of a royal house,** especially in Ionia, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 41.3}}; βασιλέων οἶκοι 'estates **of the royal house',** name of a district in Chios, Ἀθηνᾶ {{Refs|20.168.}}
**3** generally, **lord, master,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.556, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.47.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, πόλεμος πάντων β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 53; νόμος ὁ πάντων β. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 169.}}
**II** at Athens, **the second of the nine Archons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.76, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.38, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b17, “Ath.” 57, etc.; ἡ τοῦ β. στοά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2a.}}
**II.2** title of magistrates in other Greek states, as βασιλᾶες at Elis, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“GDI” 1152, compare “IG” 12(2).6 (Mytil.), etc., 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b29.}}
**II.3** at Rome, β. τῶν ἱερῶν, = **rex sacrorum,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.1, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.27.}}
**III** after the Persian war (without _Article_), **the king of Persia,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.174, +others; ἄναξ Ξέρξης β. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 5, compare 144, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 61, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, “IG” 22.141 (βασιλῆς βασιλέως ὕποχοι μεγάλου}} of the Satraps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24, compare 44, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 2.22); less frequently ὁ βασιλεύς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132, 137, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304b13; β. ὁ μέγας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188.}}
**III.2** of Alexander and his successors, usually with _Article_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.641,687, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 293, 340 (_plural_); Σέλευκος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 187}}; Ἀντίγονος Alex.III; Πτολεμαῖος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 244; Ὀσυμανδύας βασιλεὺς βασιλέων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.47}}; title used by Parthian **kings,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 38, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.6, etc.; by Antony, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 54}}; of **God,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 17.14,19.16.}}
**III.3** of the Roman **emperors,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 10.25 (Antip. Thess.); β. Ῥωμαίων “BGU” 588.10 (1st c.AD), etc.,cf. NT.1Tim.2.2, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.15.14; β. αὐτοκράτωρ “IG” 3.13 (from Hadrian), 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.6.5; without _Article_, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.32.19.}}
**IV** of any **great man,** πένησί τε καὶ βασιλεῦσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 113.}}
**IV.2** **first** or **most distinguished of any class,** Ἡρώδην τὸν β. τῶν λόγων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.10.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” II}}; **winner** at a game, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.106, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146a}}; Stoic **sage,** μόνος β. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 16}}; βασιλέως ἐγκέφαλος 'morsel fit for a king', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 5}}; β. σῦκα, name of a **choice** kind, Philem.Legal. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.76f., compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.81.}}
**V** ={συμποσίαρχος}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.622a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 4.}}
**VI** **wren,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592b27.}}
**VII** **queen-bee,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 623b9, “GA” 759a20, etc. (The form βασιλέα}} is scanned ?~X ?~X ¯ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.39}}; codices βασίλεια.)

936:
** βᾰσῐλ-εύω**,
**to be king, rule, reign,** οὐ μέν πως πάντες βασιλεύσομεν ἐνθάδ᾽ Ἀχαιοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.203; ἶσον ἐμοὶ βασίλευε 9.616}}; ἐν ὑμῖν.. βασίλευε **was king** among you, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.47; ὄφρ᾽ Ἰθάκης κατὰ δῆμον.. βασιλεύοι 22.52}}; also of a woman, ἢ βασίλευεν ὑπὸ Πλάκῳ **reigned as queen,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.425; ἡ δὲ Πύλου βασίλευε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.285}}: in _aorist_, **to have become king,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2}}: with _genitive_, **to be king of, rule over,** ἐν.. Ἰθάκῃ βασιλεύσει Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.401, etc.; βασιλεύοντος βασιλέων Ἀρσάκου “PAvrom.” 1 A 1}}: with _dative_, **to be king among,** Γιγάντεσσιν βασίλευεν {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.59; later β. ἐπὶ τὰς δύο βασιλείας LXX.1Mac.1.16}}:—_passive_, **to be governed by a king,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 576d, 576e, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284b39, etc.: c._accusative_ _cognate_, βασιλείαν πασῶν δικαιοτάτην βασιλεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 680e}}: generally, **to be governed** or **administered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.106, etc.; ὑπὸ νόμου 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.19}}: hence, **submit to the king,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 12.}}
**b** **to be** ἄρχων β. at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.5, “IG” 12.776, +others}} of other magistrates, {{Refs|“SIG” 709 (from Chersonesus), 1054 (from Samothrace).}}
**c** later ἡ βασιλεύουσα πόλις the **imperial** city, of Rome, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.98c, compare “CPHerm.” 125ii3.}}
**2** **enjoy as master,** τῶ χρυσῶ β. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.60 codices}}
**3** _absolutely_, **live royally,** β. ἐν πενίᾳ {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.101d, cf. NT.1Cor.4.8.}}
**II** causal, **appoint as king,** τινά {{LXX|LXX.Josh.9.6}}; but β. τισὶ βασιλέα **make** them **a king,**{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.8.22, 12.1.}}

937:
** βᾰσῐλ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**royal, kingly,** ποιέεις οὐδαμῶς -κά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173; β. γένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 869}}; β. [μοναρχία] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291e}}; opposed to τυραννικός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b3}}; βασιλικοὶ ἀπέβησαν proved themselves **truly kingly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.10.10; βασιλικόν [ἐστι] πράττειν μὲν εὖ, κακῶς δ᾽ ἀκούειν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.6.20; ἦθος β. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.10; τὸ β. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.18: βασιλική (i.e. τέχνη)}}, ἡ, **art of ruling,** {{Refs|Andronic.Rhod. p.574M.}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.45, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.54d}}: _superlative_ βασιλικώτατος καὶ ἄρχειν ἀξιώτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.29; -ωτάτη χάρις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 21}}. _adverb_ -κῶς, παρών **as a king, with kingly authority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14; β. ἄρχειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259b1.}}
**2** **of** or **belonging to a king,** οἱ β. **the king's friends** or **officers,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.12.10}}; ἐγκλήματα β. charges **of high-treason,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 25.3.1}}; ὀφειλήματα β. debts **to the king,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 3; β. πρόσοδοι “PPetr.” 3p.56}}; γραμματεύς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(compare 11.1) Wilcken “Chr.” 233.2 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; γεωργοί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.200 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; ὁδὸς β. the **king's** highway, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Num.20.17, “PPetr.” 3p.65 (3rd c.BC)}}; μὴ εἶναι β. ἀτραπὸν ἐπὶ γεωμετρίαν no **royal** road, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra cited in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.68F.; β. νόμος “OGI” 483.1, NT.Jam.2.8}}; αἱ β. βίβλοι the books **of Kings,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.427.}}
**3** **choice** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (βασίλειος 3), μίνδαξ 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 27.}}
**4** κάρυα β. **walnuts,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.125; καρύαι “PSI” 4.428.65 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4.b** β. κύμινον, ={ἄμι}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.62.}}
**II** as _substantive_,
**II.1** βασιλικός (i.e. γραμματεύς), ὁ, official in Egyptian νομοί, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1219.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.1.b** (i.e. οἶκος) **basilica**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2782.25 (from Aphrodisias).}}
**II.1.c** (i.e. ὄρνις) ={ἀκαλανθίς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1078.}}
**II.1.d** (i.e. ἀστήρ) ={βασιλίσκος} see, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.201.23.}}
**II.2** βασιλικὴ στοά **hall** divided into aisles by columns, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).326.18 (from Thera), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.8 (_plural_)}}; β. alone, {{Refs|“OGI” 511.15 (from Aezani)}}, Latin _basilica,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 5.1.4,6.3.9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 15, “Cat.Mi.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.26.}}
**II.3** βασιλικόν (i.e. ταμιεῖον), τό, **treasury,** εἰς τὸ β. ἀπομετρῆσαι, τελεῖν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.344.17 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.40, etc.; ὀφείλειν “PRev.Laws” 5.1, +others}}; **royal bank**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 90.29 (from Rosetta), “PRein.” 13.19, +others, “BGU” 830.18 (1st c.AD).}}
**II.3.b** (i.e. δῶμα) **palace,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.4.}}
**II.3.c** (i.e. πρόσταγμα) **royal decree,** {{LXX|LXX.Est.1.19.}}
**II.3.d** (i.e. φάρμακον) name for various remedies, ={τετραφάρμακον}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.601}}; of other compounds, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; **a plaster,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.184}}; **an eyesalve,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 12.782 (also -κός, ὁ}} **a bandage,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(1).777).}}
**II.3.e** (i.e. φυτόν) **basil, Ocimum basilicum,** {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**II.3.f** βασιλικά, τά, **communications received from kings**, {{Refs|“SIG” 333.23 (from Samos), 426.26 (from Teos)}}; also, **interests** or **revenues of the crown,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 15.4 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.256 (2nd c.BC), LXX.1Mac.10.43}}; **prerogatives,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 15.8.}}

938:
** βᾰσῐλ-ισσα**, ἡ,
={βασίλειᾰ}, **queen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 6, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 16.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 179, “Supp.epigram” 1.366.34 (Samos, 3rd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.24, “IG” 2.614b; ἡ β. τῶν μελισσῶν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.99; condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 202, but compare 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 91.}}
**2** **wife of the** ἄρχων βασιλεύς **at Athens,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.90}}:—also **βασίλιννα**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.74, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 907.}}
**3** the Roman **Empress,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.7.4, etc.}}
**4** ={βασιλεύς} VI, **PMag.Leid. V.**{{Refs|1.31.}}

939:
** βάσις** [ᾰ], εως, ἡ,
(βαίνω) **stepping, step,** and collectively, **steps,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 8, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἡσύχῳ φρενῶν βάσει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 452 (Lyric poetry); οὐκ ἔχων β.}}**power to step,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 691 (Lyric poetry)}}; τροχῶν βάσεις the **rolling** of the wheels, the **rolling** wheels, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 718}}; ἀρβύλης β. the **print** of the sandal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 532; ποίμναις τήνδ᾽ ἐπεμπίπτει βάσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 42.}}
**2** **measured stepor movement,** β. χορείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 968, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.2}}: hence, **rhythmical** or **metrical movement,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399e, “Lg.” 670d}}: in Rhet., **rhythmical close** of a sentence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6, +others}}; **clause forming transition from** πρότασις **to** ἀπόδοσις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Inv.” 1.5}}: and in Metric, **metrical unit, monometer,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263b35, “Metaphorical” 1087b36, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 11, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Prolegomena ad Hephaestionis Euchiridion” 3, Mar. Vict. p.47.3 K., etc.}}
**3** **order, sequence,** θέσις καὶ β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.10U.}}
**II** **that with which one steps, a foot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 92a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 750a4; ποδῶν β. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 837}}; θηλύπους β. their women's **feet,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 421}}; β. δίχηλος, of the ostrich, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.28.3: _absolutely_, αἱ βάσεις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.226, NT.Act.3.7; σφὶγξ εἶχε β. λέοντος 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.5.8}}; **leg,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 1.3.5; βάσεων ἀποκοπαί 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 39.}}
**III** **that whereon one stands, base, pedestal,** [κρατῆρος] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 119}}; of statues, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 705.6, etc.; τρία ἔργα.. ἐπὶ μιᾶς β. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.14, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 19; λεβήτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.88.5}}; of an engine, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 88.1, +others}}; of a column, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLond.” 3.755v6 (4th c.AD): in Medicine texts, τοῦ ἐγκεφάλου 3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in “Placita Philosophorum” 4.5.4, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 6; τραχήλου [prev. author] “Pyrrh.” 34; κοῖλαι βλεφάρων ἰοτυπεῖς βάσιες “Anthologia Graeca” 5.86 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus); αἱ ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖς β. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.27, compare 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.101(91)}}; of the heart, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.13; ἐπανόρθωσις τὴν τοῦ κενουμένου βάσιν ἀναπληροῦσα [prev. author] 1.474}}; **foundation, basement,** ῥίζα πάντων καὶ β. ἁ γᾶ ἐρήρεισται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 97e}}; so, of the soil, πεδίων σπορίμα β. {{Refs|“Hymnus ad Isim” 162.}}
**III.2** Geometry texts, **base** of a solid or plane figure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 55b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 41b15, +others}}; [κώνου] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 155; πυραμίδος 4th c.BC: Speusippus Philosophus cited in “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 63.}}
**IV** **position, fixedness,** opposed to φορά, _etymology_ of βέβαιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 437a.}}
**V** Astrology texts, ={ὡροσκόπος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 88.6, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “T.” 2, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).132.}}

940:
** βασκ-αίνω**,
_future_ **-ᾰνῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Deu.28.56}}: _aorist_ ἐβάσκηνα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD:}} Philostratus Sophista (see. below), **-ᾱνα** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 926b24:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐβασκάνθην (see. below):—**bewitch** by the evil eye, etc., {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus [same place], LXX.Deu.28.56: _metaphorically_, NT.Gal.3.1; ἐβάσκηνε πάντα.. τύχη 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.4.5}}:—_passive_, ὡς μὴ βασκανθῶσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 347; ὡς μὴ βασκανθῶ τρὶς ἔπτυσα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.39.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **malign, disparage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 174, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.19; ἄν τι δύσκολον συμβαίνῃ τοῦτο βασκαίνει [prev. author] 18.189; εἰσίν τινες.. οὓ τὸ βασκαίνειν τπέφει 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 11}}:—_passive_, ὑπὸ τῶν ἀντιτέχνων βασκανθῆναι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.7.}}
**II.2** with _dative_, **envy, grudge,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.24, etc.}}; τινί τινος **grudge** one a thing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 78.37, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.12; τινὶ ἐπί τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 78.25: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 17: τινός}}**keep to oneself,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Philops.” 35.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, μὴ βασκήνας γελάσαι καὶ ἄλλον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.20.}}

941:
** βαστάζω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.594, etc.}}: _future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1019, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 920}}; late **-άξω** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 1.45, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐβάστασα {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.405, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 437 (Lyric poetry), etc.; late ἐβάσταξα “PFay.” 122, LXX.Sir.6.25, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.8.7, epigram cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.52}}:—_passive_, _future_ βασταχθήσομαι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 1.42:}}_aorist_ ἐβαστάχθην{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Nic.Dam. p.114D., 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.59, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.693e}}: _aorist 2_ βασταγῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.68:}}_perfect_ βεβάσταγμαι (ἐμ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 14}}:—**lift up, raise,** λᾶαν βαστάζοντα.. ἀμφοτέρῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.594; ἐπεὶ μέγα τόξον ἐβάστασε 21.405; πεπτῶτα β. τινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 827, etc.}}; **lift** a veil, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1470}}:—_passive_, of sluice-gates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 81.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **lift up, exalt, ennoble,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.19; β. τινὰ χαρίτεσσιν [prev. author] “I.” 3.8.}}
**II** **bear, carry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1019, etc.; χερσὶν β. τινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1129, compare 1216; δόρυ 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 46.2 (_anapaest meter_), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.78; ὅπλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 107.}}
**II.2** **hold in one's hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 905; χεροῖν [prev. author] “Ph.” 657, compare 1127 (Lyric poetry)}}; of books, συνεχῶς β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.35U.}}:—in _passive_, **to be popular,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413b12.}}
**II.3** β. ἐν γνώμῃ **bear** in mind, **consider, weigh,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 888; φρενί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 437 (Lyric poetry)}}; β. προβούλευμα **deliberate on**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 73; βαστάσας αἱρήσομαι}}**on consideration,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 303.}}
**II.4** **bear, endure,** οὐκέτι βαστάζω τὴν σεῖο διαζυγίην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.8 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.5** **produce, yield,** of land, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 6 iii 8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** **carry off, take away,** {{NT|NT.John.20.15}}; **steal,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.15.4, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.19.9, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.59, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 16, “PTeb.” 330.7 (2nd c.AD), perhaps also in NT.John.12.6, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.46f (_passive_).}}
**III.2** in _passive_, **to be sublimated,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.198 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**IV** in Trag., **touch,** χέρα ἄνακτος.. τῇδε β. χερί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 35}}; **embrace,** σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1105.}}—Not in _Attic dialect_ Prose: _passive_ first in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus}}

942:
** βάτος**[ᾰ], ἡ,
**A** **bramble, Rubus ulmifolius,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.230, Aen. Tact. 28.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.132}}; ὁ, [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.112, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 754 (_Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Moeris), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.5.3, LXX.Exo.3.2: whence ἐπὶ τοῦ (variant{τῆς}) βάτου in NT.Mark.12.26: _feminine_, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.37, NT.Luke.20.37.}}]
**A.II** β. Ἰδαία **raspberry, Rubus Idaeus,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.38}}; ={β. ὀρθοφυής} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.18.4.}}
**A.III** β. Μοσυλῖτις, a kind of **cassia,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.13.}}
**A.IV** ={ἑλένιον}, **elecampane,** Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.28.}}

943:
** βάτος** ὁ,
the Hebrew liquid measure
**C** **bath,** = Egypt. ἀρτάβη or _Attic dialect_ μετρητής, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Esd.7.22, NT.Luke.16.6, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.2.9}}:—also **βάδος**, variant in {{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX [same place], 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

944:
** βάτρᾰχος** [βᾰ], ὁ,
**frog,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 6, 18, 59, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.131, etc.: proverbial, ὕδωρ πίνειν βάτραχος}} **a very frog** to drink, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10.3}}; βατράχοις οἰνοχοεῖν, of those who give what is not wanted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 70.5; μέλει μοι τῶν τοιούτων ἧττον τῶν ἐν τοῖς τέλμασι β. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 358a}}; χλωρὸς β., of the tree-**frog,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 15.}}
**II** ={ἁλιεύς}, a kind of **fish, fishing-frog** or **sea-angler, Lophius piscatorius,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 749a23, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.5.}}
**III** **frog of a horse's hoof,** {{Refs|Gp. 16.1.9, “Hippiatrica” 8}}: hence Astron., of the star β Centauri, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.1.}}
**IV** ἐσχάρας εἶδος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** **swelling under the tongue,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 8.39.}} —Dial. forms are cited by Grammars,
**V.1** _Ionic dialect_ βάθρακος, cited from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (probably 4.131) by Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.243, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1570.11, and found in “PLond.” 1.124.31 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; _Ionic dialect_ also βότραχος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19, βρόταχος 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 40 (as _proper name_, “GDI” 5577,5592).}}
**V.2** βράταχος {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (as _proper name_, “GDI” 5727d29).}}
**V.3** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria βρούχετος 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V.4** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus βριαγχόνη [prev. author]}}
**V.5** Pontic βάβακος {{Refs|[prev. author] Cf. βύρθακος, βρύτιχος.}}

948:
** βδελύσσομαι**,
_Attic dialect_ **βδελύττομαι**, _future_ **-ύξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.39, 41 (_active_ βδελύξειν wrongly cited by 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus)}}: _aorist_ ἐβδελύχθην {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 792, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 57, etc.; later ἐβδελυξάμην LXX.Gen.26.20, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.2.10, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.210d}}:—**feel a loathing for food**, Hp {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” cc.}}; **to be sick**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 792.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **feel a loathing at**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 586, LXX [same place], +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 57}}; β. [τραγῳδίας] {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator [same place]}}; ὠμοφαγίαν {{LXX|[prev. work]6.193c: β. ἀπό τινων LXX.Exo.1.12.}}
**II** later causal, in _active_, **cause to stink, make loathsome** or **abominable**, _future_ **-ύξω**{{LXX|LXX.Lev.20.25:}}_aorist_ ἐβδέλυξα{{LXX|LXX.Exo.5.21}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, **to be loathsome**, _future_ -υχθήσομαι{{LXX|LXX.Sir.20.8:}}_aorist_ -ύχθην{{LXX|LXX.Psa.13(14).1:}}_perfect_ ἐβδέλυγμαι{{LXX|LXX.Pro.8.7}}; οἱ ἐβδελυγμένοι **the abominable** (in _reference_ to the use of βδέλυγμα as **an idol),** {{NT|NT.Rev. 21.8}}:—this _perfect_ in causal sense, {{LXX|LXX.Pro.28.9. (Cf. βδέω.)}}

949:
** βέβαιος**, ον
(so always in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus)}}, also α, ον (see. below): (βαίνω):— **firm, steady**, κρύσταλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23; ὄχημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85d (_comparative_)}}; γῆ β. **terra firma**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.21.5}}; **steadfast, durable**, ὁμιλία.. πιστὴ καὶ βέβαιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 71; ἀρετῆς βέβαιαι.. αἱ κτήσεις μόνης [prev. author] “fragment” 194; ψῆφος βεβαία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1263; τὴν χάριν βέβαιον ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32; οὐδέπω βέβαιος ἦν ἡ σωτηρία 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.53; εἰρήνην βεβαίαν ἀγαγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.173; φιλία βέβαιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182c; βεβαίου τε καὶ καθαρᾶς ἡδονῆς γεύεσθαι [prev. author] “R.” 586a; δόξαι καὶ πίστεις βέβαιοι καὶ ἀληθεῖς [prev. author] “Ti.” 37b, etc.}}
**b** **sure, certain**, τέκμαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 456; ἄκεα [prev. author] “Eu.” 506 (Lyric poetry); τοξεύματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1086}}; πύλας β. παρέχειν make **safe, secure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67}}; βεβαιότερος κίνδυνος a **surer** game, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.39}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.124}}; βέβαιόν ἐστί τινι ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.35; τὰ παρ᾽ ἀνθρώπων αὐτῷ β. ἦν [prev. passage]; but β. παρέχειν τὰν ὠνάν}} **confirm, guarantee**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“GDI” 1867, +others (Delph.); μένειν κυρίαν καὶ β... συγχώρησιν “BGU” 1058.47 (1st c.BC).}}
**2** of persons, etc., **steadfast, constant**, φίλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 299 (_comparative_),compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.43, etc.: with}}_infinitive_, βεβαιότεροι μηδὲν νεωτεριεῖν **more certain** to make no change, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11.}}
**3** τό β. **certainty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 59c, etc.}}; but τὸ β. τῆς διανοίας **firmness, resolution**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89.}}
**3.b** **security, guarantee**, τὸ δημόσιον β. {{Refs|“IG” 12.189.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-ως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 15; β. κλῃστόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17; β. οἰκεῖσθαι [prev. author] 1.2}}; ἔχειν, γνῶναι, δημοκρατεῖσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.41, 39, 10.4}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, οἰκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8}}; **-οτέρως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.60, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.11}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91.}}

950:
** βεβαι-όω**,
**confirm, establish, make good**, τοῖς δικασταῖς τὴν δόξαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53b; δωρεὰν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.18; εἴτε δεξιὰς δοῖεν ἐβεβαίουν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2, etc.}}; ἔργῳ βεβαιούμενα, opposed to ἀκοῇ λεγόμενα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23}}; β. λόγον **make good** one's word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.32; β. τὴν πρᾶξιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.17}}; **treat as valid**, τὰς αὑτῶν αἰσθήσεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 1, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 24}}; β. τινί τι **secure** one **the possession of** a thing, οὐδ᾽ ἡμῖν αὐτοῖς βεβαιοῦμεν [τὴν ἐλευθερίαν] {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122; τοῖς θεοῖς βεβαιοῦντες τοὺς νόμους οὒς ἐψηφίσασθε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.29, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.30; τὴν ἀρχήν τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 22; τὸν λόγον NT.Mark.16.20}}:—_middle_, **establish for oneself, secure**, σφᾶς αὐτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33}}; τὴν ἀρχήν, τὴν φιλίαν τινός, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.10, 78}}; β. τινάς **confirm** them **in one's interest**,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 34; βασιλείαν 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.11.4; τὰ περὶ τῆς βοηθείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.51.5.}}
**2** _middle_, **secure one's ground** in argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169e}}; **confirm** oneself in an opinion, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 489a, “Prt.” 348d.}}
**3** **guarantee the validity of** a purchase, **warrant** the purchaser's title, β. τινὶ τὸ βαλανεῖον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.23, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.42, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.12, “SIG” 46.4 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; τὴν μίσθωσιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1119.47 (1st c.BC)}}: generally, β. τοὺς κανόνας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.11.24.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **determine, show itself positively**, τοῖσιν ἐνδοιαστῶς ἔχουσι.. ἐβεβαίωσε [ἡ νοῦσος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.2.}}

951:
** βεβαί-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**confirmation**, β. γνώμης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140, compare 4.87, Demetrius Astrologus Lac. “Herc.” 1012.38F., 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.486, +others, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.18, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 5}}; εἰς β. in **perpetuity**, ἡ γῆ οὐ πραθήσεται εἰς β. {{LXX|LXX.Lev.25.23.}}
**2** legal **warranty**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.249 (_plural_), “PTeb.” 311.27 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; βεβαιώσεως δίκη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.34.}}

952:
** βέβηλ-ος**, ον,
_Doric dialect_ **βέβᾱλος** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“IG” 3.3845, Ps.- Lysis Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 17.75}}: (βαίνω, βηλός):— **allowable to be trodden**, probably of ground (opposed to ἱερός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.8); καὶ πῶς β. ἄλσος ἂν ῥύοιτό μ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 509}}; ἢ πρὸς βεβήλοις ἢ πρὸς ἄλσεσιν θεῶν either on **profane ground** or.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 10; ἔς τε τἄβατα καὶ πρὸς βέβηλα [prev. author] “fragment” 88}}: hence generally, **permitted**, καὶ βέβηλα καὶ κεκρυμμένα λόγια **public, current**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 404; ἐν βεβήλῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.97}}; βέβηλα **permitted meats**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.65f.}}
**II** of persons, **unhallowed**, ={ἀμύητος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 154, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 245}}; **impure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 648; β. τε καὶ ἄγροικος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b; β. καὶ ἀνόσια ἐνθυμήματα 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.165}}: with _genitive_, **uninitiated**, τελετῆς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.298 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); ἀποδεικτικῆς μεθόδου 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 12.6}}. _adverb_ **-λως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.523.}}

953:
** βεβηλ-όω**,
**profane**, τὸ σάββατον {{LXX+NT+4th c.AD+|LXX.Exo.31.14, NT.Matt.12.5; τὰ ἀνθρώπινα 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.228d.}}
**2** **pollute, defile**, τινά {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Lev.21.9, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.25.}}

956:
** βέλος**, εος, τό,
**missile**, especially **arrow, dart**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; of the piece of rock **hurled** by the Cyclops, τόντονδε βαλὼν β. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.495}}; of an ox's leg **thrown** by one of the suitors at Ulysses, {{Refs|20.305}}; of a stool, {{Refs|17.464}}; ὑπὲκ βελέων out **of the reach of darts**, out **of shot**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.465; ἐκ βελέων 11.163; ἔξω βελῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.69, etc.; ἔξω βέλους 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.27.1, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 4}}; opposed to ἐντὸς βέλους, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.6, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.5; εἴσω β. παρελθεῖν [prev. work] 1.6.8.}}
**2** used of **any weapon**, as **a sword**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 345, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 658}}; an **axe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1159}}; **the sting** of a scorpion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 169}}; of the gad-fly, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 556.}}
**3** ἀγανὰ βέλεα of Apollo, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.759, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.280, and of Artemis,[prev. work] 5.124}}, denote **sudden, easy death** of men and women respectively; βέλος ὀξύ, of Ilithyia, **pangs** of childbirth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.269, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.29.}}
**4** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of **anything swift-darting**, Διὸς βέλη **the bolts** of Zeus, lightnings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.8, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79, etc.; Ζηνὸς ἄγρυπνον β. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 360; πύρπνουν β. [prev. work] 917}}; βέλεσι πυρπνόου ζάλης, of a storm,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 373; πάγων δύσομβρα β. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 358: _metaphorically_, ὀμμάτων β.}} **glance** of the eye, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 742}}; φίλοικτον β. a piteous **glance**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 241 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἱμέρου β. the **shaft** of love, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 649; θυμοῦ βέλη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 893 (assuming variant)}}; of arguments, πᾶν τετόξευται β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 679, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23b}}; β. τὰ ἀπὸ τοῦ στόματος, of invective, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 51.8}}; of **mental anguish** or **fear**, ἄτλατον β. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.48 (variant{δέος}) ὁ φθόνος αὐτὸς ἑαυτὸν ἑοῖς βελέεσσι δαμάζει “Anthologia Graeca” 10.111.}}]
**5** **engine of war**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 82.8}}: _plural_, **artillery**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 97.10. (Cf. βάλλω, Lith.}}**gélti** 'sting', **gēla\** 'sharp pain', OHG.**quelan** 'feel sharp pain'. Root **g[uglide]el-** 'pierce', compare δέλλιθες.)

970:
** βία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **βίη** [ῐ], ἡ: _Epic dialect_ _dative_ βίηφι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.6}}:—**bodily strength, force**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.; χειρῶν βία 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.91:—in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _periphrastic_ of strong men, βίη Ἡρακληείη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.658, where the}}_participle_ _masculine_ πέρσας follows, compare {{Refs|11.690}}; βίη Ἐτεοκληείη, Ἰφικλείη,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.386, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.290, etc.; βίη Διομήδεος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.781; also ἲς.. βίης Ἠρακληείης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 332}}: so in Lyric poetry and Trag., Πέλοπος βία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.181}}; Τυδέως βία, Πολυνείκους β., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 571, 577; φίλτατ᾽ Αἰγίσθου β. [prev. author] “Ch.” 893}}; θήρειος β., ={Κένταυροι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1059.}}
**2** personified, Κράτος Βία τε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12.}}
**3** of the mind, οὐκ ἔστι βίη φρεσίν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.45.}}
**3.b** of an argument, βίαν οὐκ ἔχειν πρὸς [τὸ] ἀποδειξαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 9.}}
**II** **act of violence**, ὕβρις τε βίη τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.329}}: mostly in _plural_, κείνων γε βίας ἀποτείσεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.117; βίας ὑποδέγμενος ἀνδρῶν 16.189; βίαι ἀνέμων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.213.}}
**II.2** βίᾳ τινός **against** one's **will, in spite of** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 746 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 79, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43, etc.; β. φρενῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 612; β. καρδίας [prev. author] “Supp.” 798}}; β. alone as _adverb_, **perforce**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.231, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.10, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 74, +others; βίῃ ἐπειρᾶτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.5}}; opposed to κατὰ φύσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 215a1; also πρὸς βίαν τινός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 5; πρὸς βίαν ἄγειν τινά [prev. author] “Pr.” 210, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 805, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 8.10 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 443, etc.}}; opposed to ἑκών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 236d; ἐκ βίας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 563, +others, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.58; ὑπὸ βίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107; ἀπὸ βίας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.51}}; of Zeus, εὐμενεῖ βία κτίσας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1068 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** in _Attic dialect_ law, **rape**, βίας δίκη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 464e; βίᾳ αἰσχύνεσθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.32.}}
**II.4** = Latin _vis_, βίας γραφή D C.37.31, compare 33; μαρτύρομαι τὴν βίαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1120.11 (3rd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _jyā´ jiyā´_ 'preponderating power', **jināti** 'oppress'.)

971:
** βῐάζω**,
**constrain**, _active_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἦ μάλα δή με βιάζετε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.297; ἐβίασε τὴν γυναῖκά μου 5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 29: _absolutely_, εἰ πάνυ ἐβίαζον}} if **they used force**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.24; compare below 1.2}}:—_passive_, _future_ βιασθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.5.9:}}_aorist_ ἐβιάσθην, _perfect_ βεβίασμαι (see. below):—**to be hard pressed** or **overpowered**, βελέεσσι βιάζεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.589}}; βιάζετο γὰρ βελ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 15.727; βιασθέντες λύᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.14; νόσῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 20 (= “Tragica Adespota” 70)}}; **to be forced** or **constrained** to do, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 890: with _accusative_ _cognate_, βιάζομαι τάδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 66, compare 1073; βιασθείς [prev. author] “El.” 575; ἐπεὶ ἐβιάσθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44; ὑπό τινος [prev. author] 1.2}}; opposed to ἀδικεῖσθαι,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 77; βιασθεὶς ἄκων ἔπραξεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.16; ἵνα ἢ συγχωρήσωσιν.. ἢ βιασθῶσιν [prev. author] 18.175; βιαζόμενος ὑπό τινος ἐξήμαρτεν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.5}}; βεβιασμένοι **forcibly made slaves**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.12}}; πόλεις βεβ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 5.2.23: βιαζόμενος ὑπὸ τῆς παρούσης ἀπορίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67}}; τὸ βιασθέν **those who are forced**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1255a11}}; of things, τοὔνειδος ὀργῇ βιασθέν **forced from one** by anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 524}}; τὸ βεβιασμένον **forced** to fit a hypothesis, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1082b2}}; βεβ. σχήματα **forced** figures of speech, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 33, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 36.}}
**2** _active_, **make good, suffice to discharge** a debt, {{Refs|“PFlor.” 56.13.}}
**II** more frequently βιάζομαι, _aorist_ _middle_ ἐβιασάμην, _perfect_ βεβίασμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.206, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 63, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.45}}:—**overpower by force, press hard**, ἦ μάλα δή σε βιάζεται ὠκὺς Ἀχιλλεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.229, etc.}}; β. τοὺς πολεμίους **dislodge** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.5}}; β. νόμους **to do** them **violence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53}}; βιασάμενος ταῦτα πάντα **having broken through** all these restraints, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.52}}; β. γυναῖκα **force** her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1092}}; opposed to πείθειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.32}}; β. αὑτόν **lay violent hands on** oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c, 61d}}; β. τινά, with _infinitive_, **force** one to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.1}}; τί με βιάζεσθε λέγει; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 44: with}}_infinitive_ omitted, β. τὰ σφάγια **force** the victims [**to be favourable**], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41; β. ἄστρα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.9: with double _accusative_, αὐδῶ πόλιν σε μὴ β. τόδε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1047.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **carry by force**, βιάσασθαι τὸν ἔκπλουν **force** an exit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.72; τὴν ἀπόβασιν [prev. author] 4.11: with _accusative_ _neuter_, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.12.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **act with violence, use force**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1010, “Ag.” 1509 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1160, etc.; πρὸς τὸ λαμπρὸν ὁ φθόνος βιάζεται “Tragica Adespota” 547.12}}; opposed to δικάζομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; β. διὰ φυλάκων **force one's way**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.83}}; β. ἐς τὸ ἔξω, β. εἴσω,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 69, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.69; δρόμῳ β. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.63}}: with _infinitive_, β. πρὸς τὸν λόφον ἐλθεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.79}}; βιαζόμενοι βλάπτειν **using every effort** to hurt me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.16}}; but βιαζόμενοιμὴ ἀποδιδόναι refusing **with violence** to repay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.12: especially in}}_participle_, ἵνα βιασάμενοι ἐκπλεύσωσι may sail out **by forcing their way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67; συνεξέρχονται βιασάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.11}}; ἐπὶ μᾶλλον ἔτι β. (of a famine) **grow worse and worse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94.}}
**II.4** **contend** or **argue vehemently**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 246b; β. τὸ μὴ ὂν ὡς ἔστι κατά τι [prev. work] 241d}}: _absolutely_, **persist in assertion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.205.}}

972:
** βίαιος** [ῐ], α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399a, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.33}}: (βία):— **forcible, violent**: _adjective_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἔρδειν ἔργα βίαια 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.236}}, _adverb_ twice, **by force, perforce**, κατέδουσι βιαίως οἶκον Ὀδυσσῆος {{Refs|2.237; γυναιξὶ παρευνάζεσθε βιαίως 22.37}}; frequently in all writers, ἔργα β. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1343; νόμος ἄγει δικαιῶν τὸ βιαιότατον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 169}}; of persons, βιαιότατος τῶν πολιτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36; χρόνος καταψήχει καὶ τὰ βιαιότατα 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 176}}; β. θάνατος a **violent** death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.170, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566b, etc.; β. νόσος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1140 (Lyric poetry); β. ἄνεμος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 370b9; ἐπάρδευσις 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.44U. (_comparative_)}}; ὁ πόλεμος β. διδάσκαλος teaches **by violence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; δίκη βιαίων an action **for forcible rescue**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}}; τοῖς β. or τῶν βιαίων ἔνοχος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914e}}; βιαίων [ἐγκαλεῖ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.33; τὰ [περὶ] τῶν βιαίων [prev. passage]; συναλλάγματα β., λαθραῖα}}, **obligationes ex delicto**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131a8; κλοπαῖα καὶ β. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 934c}}. _adverb_ βιαίως, ἀποθανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.26}}; β. σέλμα σεμνὸν ἡμένων **in their irresistible might**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 182 (Lyric poetry)}}; χαλεπῶς καὶ β. by struggling and **forcing their way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23}}; **firmly**, σχεδίας β. ζεύξαντες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.46.1}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 821 (Lyric poetry); πρὸς τὸ β. [prev. author] “Ag.” 130; ἐκ τοῦ βιαιοτάτου 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.36.}}
**2** especially of magic, β. τέχνη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.33.}} _adverb_ βιαίως, σοφός **a wizard**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.2.}}
**II** _passive_, **forced, constrained**, opposed to ἑκούσιος, πράξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 603c}}; β. κίνησις, ={παρὰ φύσιν κ}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 254a9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 64d}}; τὸ β., ={οὗ ἔξωθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ μηδὲν συμβαλλομένου τοῦ βιασθέντος} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110b15}}; ἡ β. τροφή, of the diet of athletes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1338b41; πόνοι μὴ β. [prev. work] 1335b9}}; ὁ χρημ.ατιστὴς (i.e. βίος) β. τίς ἐστιν, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] “EN” 1096a6; βιαιότερος λόγος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.191d.}} _adverb_ -ως, ={παρὰ φύσιν, κινεῖσθαι} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 253b34}}: _comparative_ **-οτέρως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).19.}}
**II.2** ={βιαιοθάνατος}, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.332.}}

975:
** βιβλίον**
or **βυβλίον**, τό, **strip of** βύβλος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.4}}: hence, **paper, document**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 3.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 974, etc.; τὸ β. τοῦ ψηφίσματος “IG” 22.1.61}}; β. ἀποστασίου **notice** of divorce, {{NT|NT.Matt.19.7.}}
**2** ={βιβλίδιον}, **petition** to the Government, = Latin _libellus_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 422 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 86.16 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**3** ={δέλτος}, **tablet**, {{LXX|LXX.Tob.7.14.}}
**II** **book**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 304, Theognetus Comicus 1.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26d, etc.; μέγα β. ἴσον τῷ μεγάλῳ κακῷ 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 359.}}
**II.2** **book** as the division of a work, ἐν τῷ πρώτῳ β. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2 “Praef.”, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.329, etc.}}
**II.3** τὰ β. **place in which books are kept, library**, ἀνεθήκατε εἰς τὰ β. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 37.8.}}
**II.4** τὰ β. τὰ ἅγια the sacred **books** or {{LXX|“Scriptures”, LXX.1Mac.12.9; τὰ β. τοῦ νόμου [prev. work] 1.56.}}

