979:
** βίος**[ῐ], ὁ,
**life**, i. e. not animal life (ζωή), but **mode of life** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|compare (εἰ χρόνον τις λέγοι ψυχῆς ἐν κινήσει μετα βατικῇ ἐξ ἄλλου εἰς ἄλλον βίον ζωὴν εἶναι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.11)}}, **manner of living** (mostly therefore of men, see {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Ammonius Epigrammaticus p.32 V.}}; but also of animals, διεχώριζον ζῴων τε βίον δένδρων τε φύσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.6, etc.; also ζῆν φυτοῦ βίον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 736b13); ζώεις δ᾽ ἀγαθὸν βίον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.491; ἐμὸν βίον ἀμφιπολεύειν 18.254; αἰῶνα βίοιο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 161; τὸν μακρὸν τείνειν βίον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 537 (Lyric poetry); ὁ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν β. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1364; βίον διαγαγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 439; τελεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1114; διατελεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.45}}; διέρχεσθαι βίου τέλος uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).5; τελευτᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.84; ὑπ᾽ ἄλλου τελευτῆσαι β. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 870e; ἐπειδὰν τοῦ ἀνθρωπίνου βίου τελευτήσω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.17; τέρμα βίου περᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1530; ὁδὸς βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21; διὰ βίου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a37}}; proverbial, ὁ ἐπὶ Κρόνου βίος 'the Golden Age', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ath.” 16.7; so Ταρτησσοῦ β. 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 10.11; β. ζωῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 982a (compare βιοτή); ζῆν θαλάττιον β. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 100; ἀμέριμνον ζῆν β. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 92.8; λαγὼ β. ζῆν δεδιὼς καὶ τρέμων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.263; σκληρὸς τῷ β. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 66}}: rarely in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 116.6 and 11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 855}}; τίνες καὶ πόσοι εἰσὶ β.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095b15, “Pol.” 1256a20.}}
**2** in Poets sometimes ={ζωή}, βίον ἐκπνέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1517 (Lyric poetry); ἀποψύχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1031; φείδεσθαι βίου [prev. author] “Ph.” 749; νοσφίζειν τινὰ βίου [prev. work] 1427, etc.}}
**3** **lifetime**, ἐς τὸν ἅπαντα ἀνθρώπων β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109; τῶν ἐπὶ τοῦ σοῦ β. γεγονότων λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a, compare “PMagd.” 18.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** **livelihood, means of living** (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus βίοτος), βίος ἐπηετανός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 31, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.10}}; τὸν βίον κτᾶσθαι, ποιεῖσθαι, ἔχειν ἀπό τινος, to make one's **living** off, to **live** by a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.11; ἀπεστέρηκας τὸν βίον τὰ τόξ᾽ ἑλών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 931, compare 933, 1282; κτᾶσθαι πλοῦτον καὶ βίον τέκνοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 450; πλείον᾽ ἐκμοχθεῖν β. [prev. work] 451}}; β. πολύς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 861; ὀλίγος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 751; βίον κεκτημένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 99.4}}; ὁ ῐδιος β. private **property**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“AJA” 17.29 (1st c.BC),compare “SIG” 762.40, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 30.170}}; β. Δημήτριος, = **corn**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 44.}}
**III** **the world we live in**, 'the world', οἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ β., opposed to **the philosophers**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.49; simply ὁ βίος [prev. author] “P.” 1.211}}; ὁ β. ὁ κοινός{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 237; μυθικὰς ὑποθέσεις ὧν μεστὸς ὁ β. ἐστί 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.226}}; ἐκκαθαίρειν τὸν β., of Hercules, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 13.1; τὸν βίον μιμούμενοι, of comic poets, Scholia Heph. p.115C.}}; also, 'the public', ἵνα ὁ β. εἰδῇ τίνα δεῖ μετακαλεῖσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}
**IV** **settled life**, almost, = **abode**, ἐν τῇ Θρᾳκίᾳ νήσῳ βίους ἱδρύσαντο {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.68, compare 72.}}
**V** **a life, biography**, as those of {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus, “Thes.” 1, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.180.}}
**VI** **caste**, διεῖλε τὸ πλῆθος εἰς τέτταρας β. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.7.1.}}
**VII** **wine made from partly dried unripe grapes**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 14.77.}}
**VIII** Astrology texts, **the second region**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “L.” 2.}} (Cf. Sanskrit _jīv´s_ 'alive', **j[imacracute]vati** 'live', Latin _uīvus_, etc.)

980:
** βῐόω**, βιοῖ
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 558a20; βιοῦσι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 200, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b2; βιοῦν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 238, etc.}}; _participle_ βιοῦντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566b24}}; _subjunctive_ βιῶσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 15.2}}: _imperfect_ ἐβίουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.48}}: _future_ βιώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 784, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 699, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 399; later βιώσω [prev. author] “Mon.” 270, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.119}}: _aorist 1_ ἐβίωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.163, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 585a21}}; but in earlier writers _aorist 2_ is more used, ὀβίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.71, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.1 codices}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ βιώτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.429}}; _subjunctive_ βιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872c; _optative_ βιῴην [prev. author] “Ti.” 89c}}, variant for{βιοίη} in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 512c}}; _infinitive_ βιῶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.174, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.174, etc.}}; _participle_ βιούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.10, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53, +others}}: _perfect_ βεβίωκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.27 and 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d, etc.}}:—_middle_, βιόομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.177, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180a17}}: for _aorist_ _middle_ see at {βιώσκομαι}:— _passive_, _future_ βιωθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.30:}}_perfect_ βεβίωμαι (see. below).—In early writers _present_ and _imperfect_ are mostly supplied by ζάω: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has only _aorist_ 2:—**live, pass one's life** (opposed to ζάω, **live, exist**), βέλτερον ἢ ἀπολέσθαι ἕνα χρόνον ἠὲ βιῶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.511, compare 10.174; ἄλλος μὲν ἀποφθίσθω, ἄλλος δὲ βιώτω 8.429; βίον βιοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 188a, etc.}}; β. παρανόμως, μετρίως, ἐνδόξως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.24, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.145f: with _neuter_ _pronoun_, ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ὧ αὐτὸς βεβίωκεν ἄρξομαι}}.. from the very **actions of** his own **life**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.130:}} —_passive_, τὰ σοὶ κἀμοὶ βεβιωμένα **the actions of** our **life**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]265,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.7, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.1}}; τὰ πεπραγμένα καὶ βεβ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.53; τοιούτων ὄντων ἃ τῷ βδελυρῷ τούτῳ.. βεβίωται [prev. author] 21.151; ἐπιτηδευμάτων οἷα τούτῳ βεβίωται [prev. author] 22.78}}; ὅ γε βεβιωμένος [βίος] {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.200}}; _impersonal_, βεβίωταί [μοι] I **have lived**, Latin _vixi_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 12.2.2, 14.21.3}}: —_middle_ in _active_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.177, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180a17.}}
**2** **survive**, ἐβίω καυθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.16.—βιόμεσθα (as if from βίομαι) is found “hymnus ad Apollinem” 528 and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ βίονται}} Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phlegon Trallianus Paradoxographus “Miracula” 2, compare βέομαι.}}

982:
** βῐ-ωτικός**, ή, όν,
**fil for life, lively**, τὴν διάνοιαν β. καὶ εὐμήχανος, ={βιομήχανος}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 616b27; _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 332 (who condemns the word)}}, ={χρήσιμος ἐν τῷ βίῳ}, as in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus 6.12.}}
**II** **of** or **pertaining to life**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.73.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.159; χάριτες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.142b; ἀηδίαι 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.30}}; ἡ -κή (i.e. τέχνη) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.61}}; τὰ β. κριτήρια, opposed to λογικά, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.15; μέριμναι β. NT.Luke.21.34; β. φροντίς 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21}}.; β. σύμβολα **business** documents, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 52.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; β. θρησκεία **popular** superstition {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (βίος 111), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4; ὁ β. νόμος 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.26 at Title Page}}; τὰ β.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 3, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.679d.}} _adverb_ -κῶς **in the tone of common life**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 629}}; **in popular language**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.269.}}
**II.2** βιωτικά, τά, **victuals,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 125.11 (1st c.AD)}}; ἡ β. ἀγορά {{Refs|“BCH” 44.74 (from Lagina).}}

984:
** βλάπτω**,
_future_ **-ψω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 704, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔβλαψα, _Epic dialect_ βλάψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.774}}: _aorist 2_ ἔβλᾰβον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.509}}: _perfect_ βέβλᾰφα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.180, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.26.2, ἔβλαφα (κατ-) “IG” 7.303.51 (from Oropus)}}:—_passive_, _future_ βλᾰβήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77e, “Grg.” 475d, “Hp.Mi.” 373a; βεβλάψομαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 16: also}}_future_ _middle_ βλάψομαι (in _passive_ _signification_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, 6.64}}: _aorist 1_ ἐβλά-φθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.331, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἐβλάβην [ᾰ], _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔβλαβεν, βλάβεν, {{Refs|23.461, 545}}, _participle_ βλᾰβείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 120 (Lyric poetry)}}(_aorist_ _middle_ βλάψαντο only in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.466)}}: _perfect_ βέβλαμμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.660, etc.}}:—**disable, hinder**, μή τιν᾽ ἑταίρων βλάπτοι ἐλαυνόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.22; βλάψας δέ μοι ἵππους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.571}}; β. πόδας **disable** the feet for running, **lame** them, {{Refs|[prev. work] 782}}:—_passive_, ζωὸν ἕλε βλαφθέντα κατὰ κλόνον **entangled** in the mêlée, {{Refs|16.331}}; ὄζῳ ἔνι βλαφθέντε μυρικίνῳ [the horses] **caught** in a branch, {{Refs|6.39}}; βλάβεν ἅρματα καὶ ταχε᾽ ἵππω chariots and horses **were stopped**, {{Refs|23.545}}; Διόθεν βλαφθέντα βέλεμνα **stopped, baffled** by Zeus, {{Refs|15.489, compare 484}}; βεβλαμμένος ἦτορ **stopped** in his life (assuming variant), {{Refs|16.660.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **hinder from**, τόν γε θεοὶ βλάπτουσι κελεύθου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.195; οὐδέ τις αὐτὸν βλάπτειν οὔτ᾽ αἰδοῦς οὔτε δίκης ἐθέλει 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.40 (repeated in 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 938):}} —_passive_,βλαβέντα λοισθίων δρόμων **arrested in** its last course, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 120 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** of the mind, **distract, pervert, mislead**, of the gods, τὸν δέ τις ἀθανάτων βλάψε φρένας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.178, compare “Tragica Adespota” 455: with _accusative_ _person_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.15, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.14}}; so of Ate, φθάνει δέ τε πᾶσαν ἐπ᾽ αἶαν βλάπτουσ᾽ ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.507}}; also of wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.294}}; βλαφθείς, Latin _mente captus_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.512: so with _genitive_, ἥ τε [Περσεφόνη].. βλάπτουσα νόοιο 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 705; νόου βεβλαμμένος ἐσθλοῦ [prev. author] 223.}}
**III** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **damage, hurt**, οἷσι μὴ βλάψῃ θεός (i.e. τὰ τέκνα) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 661, etc.}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, πλείω β. τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33; μείζω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30c; ἄλλο τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.22, etc.}}:—_passive_, μεγάλα βεβλάφθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.30; βεβλαμμένος τὸν ὀφθαλμόν “PStrassb.” 52.2 (2nd c.AD), etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, β. τοὺς βίους μείζους βλάβας}} **do** greater **mischiefs to**.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 12.4: with double _accusative_, β. τὴν πόλιν τοὺς ὑπολοίπους}} **rob** her of.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.131:}} —_passive_, τοσούσδε βλαβῆναι τὴν πόλιν **lose** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hann.” 28}}; τὸ βλαβέν, ={βλάβη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933e.}}
**III.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, β. λόγον **mar** the prophecy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.94}}; τοὺς ὅρκους **violate** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 148}}. (βλαπ-μλαπ- (**mḷq[uglide]**), cf. Sanskrit _marcáyati_ 'injure', **mṛktás** 'wounded', Latin _mulco_ 'maltreat'.)

985:
** βλαστάνω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 611, etc.}} (later **βλαστέω**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.17.4 (interpolated in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 589}}, corrupt in _passive_ -ουμένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 255.7))}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ βλαστάνεσκε (variant{βλάστεσκεν}) {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 546}}: _future_ βλαστήσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.2, βλαστήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 12}}: _aorist 2_ ἔβλαστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 341, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἐβλάστησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 21.10, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 26, etc. (not in _Attic dialect_)}}: _perfect_ βεβλάστηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Oss.” 12, 5th c.BC: Hellanicus Historicus 1 (b) 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.684c; ἐβλάστηκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 594 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 329}}: _pluperfect_ ἐβεβλαστήκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.26}}:—**bud, sprout, grow**, properly of plants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 594, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 697 (Lyric poetry), Th.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1124, etc.}}; ἦ βλαστὸς οὐκ ἔβλαστε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 341; εἰς ἴα σου.., καὶ ἐς κρίνα βλαστήσειεν ὀστέα “IG” 14.607 (from Carales).}}
**2** _metaphorically_in Poets, **shoot forth, come to light**, βλάστε νᾶσος ἐξ ἁλός, of Rhodes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.69}}; of children, **to be born**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 8.7}}; ἀνθρώπου φύσιν βλαστών **born** in man's nature, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 761, compare “OT” 1376, “El.” 440; ἄργυρος κακὸν νόμισμ᾽ ἔβλαστε [prev. author] “Ant.” 296; β. δ᾽ ἀπιστία [prev. author] “OC” 611; μέγιστ᾽ ἔβλαστε νόμιμα [prev. author] “El.” 1095 (Lyric poetry)}}; not common in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 498b, “Phdr.” 251b, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.28.}}
**II** causal, **make to grow, produce, propagate**, in _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 54: _metaphorically_, β. χάριτες εὔνοιαν Aristeas 230}}: mostly _aorist 1_ ἐβλάστησα {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1131; θεὸς.. ἄμπελον ἐβλάστησεν 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 36.356, cf. LXX.Gen.1.11, LXX.Num.17.8}}:—_passive_, βλαστηθείς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.667.}}

986:
** βλαστός**, ὁ,
**shoot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, 8.55, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.6.3. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 795a4, “GA” 731a9, “POxy.” 1692.20}}; **bud**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.4, “CP” 1.11.4}}; **embryo, germ**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HP” 8.2.2}}; ὁ τοῦ β. καιρός, i. e. Spring, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.82.}}
**2** **blossom**, β. κρίνου {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.24.}}
**II** **offsprmg**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 341, “epigram Gr.” 224 (from Samos).}}

987:
** βλασφημ-έω**,
_perfect_ βεβλασφήμηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.10}}:—**speak profanely of sacred things**, εἰς θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381e}}; **offer rash prayers**, {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus2.149c}}; β. κατά τινος **utter imprecations** against, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.180.}}
**2** **speak ill** or **to the prejudice of** one, **slander**, περὶ τῆς ἐμῆς διατριβῆς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.2, cf. D.[same place], [prev. work]82; β. κατά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.65, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 44; ὅσα εἰς ἡμᾶς ἐβλασφήμησαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.3; β. τινά 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 71.6, NT.Luke.23.39, etc.: _absolutely_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.8}} O.:—_passive_, **to have evil spoken of one**, βεβλασφημημένους {{NT+1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Vit.” p.12 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, cf. NT.1Cor.10.30.}}
**3** **speak impiously** or **irreverently of God, blaspheme**, εἰς τὸν Κύριον {{LXX+NT|LXX.Dan.3.29(96); εἰς τὸ πνεῦμα τὸ ἅγιον NT.Mark.3.29}}; εἰς τὰ θεῖα Vett.{{LXX+NT|Val. 58.12; τοὺς θεούς [prev. author] 67.20: _absolutely_, LXX.2Mac.10.34, +others, NT.Matt.9.3.}}

988:
** βλασφημ-ία**, ἡ,
**word of evil omen, profane speech**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.26}}; βλασφημίαν ἐφθέγξατο, at a sacrifice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1189; εἴ τις παραστὰς τοῖς βωμοῖς βλασφημοῖ β. πᾶσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800c; πᾶσαν β. ἱερῶν καταχέουσι [prev. work]d.}}
**2** **defamation, slander**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 177, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.36, 18.95; β. ποιεῖσθαι εἴς τινα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.167, cf. NT.Eph.4.31}}; ὅλας ἁμάξας βλασφημιῶν whole cart-loads **of abuse**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Eun.” 2.}}
**3** **irreverent speech against God, blasphemy**, ἡ εἰς τὸ θεῖον β. {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 715: in _plural_, LXX.Eze.35.12, +others; τοῦ Πνεύματος}}**against..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.12.31; πρὸς τὸν θεόν NT.Rev.13.6.}}

989:
** βλάσφημ-ος**, ον,
**speaking ill-omened words, evil-speaking**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b11}}: with _genitive_, **against**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1100d, etc.}}
**2** of words, **slanderous, libellous**, δέδοικα μὴ βλάσφημον μὲν εἰπεῖν ἀληθὲς δ᾽ ᾖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 4 (_superlative_)}}. _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.19, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.126.}}
**3** **blasphemous**, ἔθνη {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Mac.10.4; ῥήματα NT.Act.6.11; λαλεῖν βλάσφημα NT.Rev.13.5}}: _substantive_, **blasphemer**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Mac.9.28, NT.1Tim.1.13, etc.}}

991:
** βλέπω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.8, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἔβλεπον {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 67}}: _future_ βλέψομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.98}}, _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ βλεψεῖσθαι {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|“IG” 4.951.75 (Epid.), later βλέψω LXX.Isa.6.9, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.46J., etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔβλεψα (see. below): _perfect_ βέβλεφα (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Antip.Stoic. 3.254 (codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes)}}; βέβλοφα (ἐμ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.42.21 (2nd c.AD):}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐβλέφθην (προσ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.68of:}}_perfect_ βέβλεμμαι to be supplied in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.409c, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1401.16:}} —chiefly in _present_ and _aorist_ _active_ in early writers: _middle_ (except _future_) and _passive_ only late:—**see, have the power of sight** (distinct from ὁρῶ **perceive, be aware of**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.37), opposed to τυφλός εἰμι, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 302, compare 348, “OC” 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 15, etc.; βλέποντες ἔβλεπον μάτην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 447; βλέποντας ἀθλιωτάτους 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 234}}; μὴ βλέπων ὁ μάντις ᾖ lest he **see too clearly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 747}}; ὁ βλέπων **the seer**, Hebraism in {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.9.9}}; ὀλίγον βλέπων short- **sighted,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 39.9 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **look**, βλέφ᾽ ὧδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 402; ἐπὶ ἐμοί [prev. author] “Aj.” 345 (assuming variant); εἴς τι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 802; ἐπί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; εἰς τὰ τούτων πρόσωπα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; πῶς βλέπων**;** with what **face?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 110; ὄμμασιν ποίοις β.; [prev. author] “OT” 1371; β. ἅμα πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 428d: with}} _adverb_, φιλοφρόνως, ἐχθρῶς β. πρός τινας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.4, “Smp.” 4.58: frequently followed by noun in _accusative_, φόβον β.}} **look** terror, i. e. to **look** terrible, Θυιὰς ὣς φόβον βλέπων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 498}}; Comedy texts, ἔβλεψε νᾶπυ **looked** mustard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 631; ἀνδρεῖον.. καὶ βλέποντ᾽ ὀρίγανον [prev. author] “Ra.” 603; βλεπόντων κάρδαμα [prev. author] “V.” 455}}; πυρρίχην βλέπων **looking like** a war-dancer, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 1169}}; αἴκειαν βλέπων **looking like** one disgraced, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1671}}; σκύτη β., of a slave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 282, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 643; β. ἀπιστίαν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 309: also followed by}} _adjective_, μέγα β.{{Refs 7th c.BC+|uncertain in 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 19; φθονερὰ β. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.39; γλίσχρον β. 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 10.16, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 479, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 309c}}: by _infinitive_, ἁρπάζειν β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 181; ὀρχεῖσθαι μόνον β. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 97}}: by _participle_ _neuter_, τί πεφροντικὸς βλέπεις **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773.}}
**II.2** β. ἐς **look** to, **rely** on, εἰς ἔργον οὐδὲν γιγνόμενον βλέπετε {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.8; ἐς θεούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 923; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστὶν εἰς ὅ τι βλέπω [prev. author] “Aj.” 514}}; ἔς σε δὴ βλέπω, ὅπως.. in the hope that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 954}}: _metaphorically_ also, **have regard** to, ἡ πολιτεία β. εἰς πλοῦτον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293b14}}; of aspects, οἰκίαι πρὸς μεσημβρίαν βλέπουσαι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.9; πέτρα βλέπουσα πρὸς νότον 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.1.4; κάτω γὰρ οἱ ὀδόντες βλέπουσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 502a1; ὅταν τὸ οὖθαρ βλέπῃ κάτω [prev. work] 523a2.}}
**II.3** **look longingly, expect, propose**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 376, “V.” 847.}}
**II.4** **look to** a thing, **beware**, ἀπό τινος {{NT|NT.Mark.8.15}}; τι {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Phil.3.2: with _accusative_ _person_, β. ἑαυτούς NT.Mark.13.9; βλέπε σα (υ) τὸν ἀπὸ τῶν Ἰουδαίων “BGU” 1079.24 (1st c.AD)}}; β. ἵνα.. {{NT|NT.1Cor.16.10}}; β. ἑαυτοὺς ἵνα μὴ.. {{NT|NT.2John.8; βλέπετε τί ἀκούετε NT.Mark.4.24.}}
**III** _transitive_, **see, behold**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1042, etc.}}; ἐξ αὑτοῦ βλεπόμενον self-**evident**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.184}}; τὰ βλεπόμενα **the visible universe**, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.13.7.}}
**III.2** ζῇ τε καὶ β. φάος **sees** the light of day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 299, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 60; νόστιμον β. φάος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 261}}; βλέποντα νῦν μὲν ὄρθ᾽ ἔπειτα δὲ σκότον (i. e. being blind) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 419: hence, without φάος}}, **to be alive**, ζῶντα καὶ βλέποντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 677; βλέποντα κἀμπνέοντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 883, compare 1349, “Aj.” 962}}; of things, ἀληθῆ καὶ βλέποντα **actually existing**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 844.}}
**III.3** **look for**, μεῖζόν τι β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 172c.}}
**III.4** Astrology texts of signs equidistant from the tropical points, **to be in aspect**, β. ἄλληλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 36, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.9. (βλέφαρα occurs in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, but not βλέπω except in Batrachomyomachia [same place])}}

994:
** βοάω**,
_Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ βοάᾳ, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ βοόωσιν, _participle_ βοόων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.394, 17.265, 15.687}}: _Ionic dialectimperfect_ βοάασκε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.588}}: _future_ βοήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ βοάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1154 (Lyric poetry); later βοήσω 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.792, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.32 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator), etc. (βοάσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1447}} (Lyric poetry) is _aorist_ _subjunctive_): _aorist_ ἐβόησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.15, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 772, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ βόησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.847}}; _Doric dialect_ βόασα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.14}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἔβωσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.337, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.146, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 1, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.23; sometimes in Comedy texts, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1155}}: _perfect_ βεβόηκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.11}}:—_middle_, βοώμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1228 (perhaps _passive_)}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ βοήσατο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.465}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἐβώσατο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.60}}; _participle_ βοησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 25.3}}:—_passive_, _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ ἐβώσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.131}}: _perfect_ βεβόημαι {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.138 (from Aceratus)}}, _Ionic dialect_ _participle_ βεβωμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.39}}: _pluperfect_ ἐβεβόητο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.11.3}}:—**cry aloud, shout**, ὀξὺ βοήσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.89; ὅσσον τε γέγωνε βοήσας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.294; πᾶσα γὰρ πόλις βοᾷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1106 (Lyric poetry); ὡς δράκων β. [prev. author] “Th.” 381; β. γραμμάτων ἐν ξυλλαβαῖς [prev. work] 468}}; οἱ βοησόμενοι **men ready to shout** (in the ἐκκλησία), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.20}}; ὁ δῆμος ἐβόησευ.., of acclamations, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 41.19 (3rd-4th c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.1, +others, “IG” 12(9).906 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD).}}
**2** of things, **roar, howl**, as the wind and waves, οὔτε.. κῦμα τόσον βοάᾳ ποτὶ χέρσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.394}}; **resound, echo**, ἀμφὶ δέ τ᾽ ἄκραι ἠϊόνες βοόωσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|17.265; βοᾷ δὲ πόντιος κλύδων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 431 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; βοᾷ δ᾽ ἐν ὠσὶ κέλαδος **rings**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 605}}; τὸ πρᾶγμα φανερόν ἐστιν, αὐτὸ γὰρ βοᾷ it **proclaims** itself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 921; φαίνεται αὐτὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα βοᾶν ὡς ἑλκόμενα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1091a10.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **call to one, call on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.36, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 205 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.41:}} — _middle_ βοησάμενοι δαίμονας Ant.Lib. [prev. cited]
**II.2** with _accusative_, **call for, shout out for**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 772; β. τὴν βοήθειαν “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.2.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, β. βοάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1153 (Lyric poetry)}}; β. μέλος, ἰωάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 976, “Ph.” 216 (Lyric poetry); β. λοιγόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 402 (Lyric poetry); ἄλγος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1310 (Lyric poetry): with double _accusative_, βοάσαθ᾽ ὑμέναιον ἀοιδαῖς ἰαχαῖς τε νύμφαν}} **sound aloud** the bridal hymn in honour of the bride, {{Refs|[prev. work] 335 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἔλεγον ἰήϊον ἐβόα κίθαρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps. fragment” 3(1).iii 10.}}
**II.4** **noise abroad, celebrate**, ἡ ῥάφανος ἣν ἐβοᾶτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 15.7; πρήγματα βεβωμένα ἀνὰ Ἰωνίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.39; ἐβώσθησαν ἀνὰ τὴν Ἑλλάδα [prev. author] 6.131; οἱ βοηθέντες ἐπὶ χρήμασι 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.155}}; βεβοῆσθαι ἀπὸ τοῦ Μαραθῶνος, ἐκ τῶν ἀδικημάτων, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Decl.” 11.18, 5.53.}}
**II.5** with _infinitive_, **cry aloud** or **command in aloud noice** to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1287, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 297 (Lyric poetry); βοᾶν τινι ἄγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.12; ἐβόων ἀλλήλοις μὴ θεῖν [prev. work] 19}}; also, **cry aloud** that.., Epicrat. 11.31 (_anapaest meter_); β. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.1, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 125.5.}}
**II.6** _passive_, **to be filled with sound**, πᾶσαν δὲ χρὴ γαῖαν βοᾶσθαι ὑμνῳδίαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1434}}; **to be deafened**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1228. (Cf. βοή.)}}

995:
** βοή**,
_Doric dialect_ **βοά**, ἡ, **loud cry, shout**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly **battle-cry**, βοὴν ἀγαθός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.408, +others}}; βοᾶς δ᾽ ἔτι μηδ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ εἴη let there be not even the name **of war**, {{Refs|Thcoc. 16.97}}; later of prayer, Ἑλληνικὸν νόμισμα θυστάδος β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 269}}; κακοφάτιδα β. **cry** of mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 936 (Lyric poetry); β. καὶ οἶκτος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.48; κραυγὴ καὶ β. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.9}}; also, **song** of joy, ἴτω ξύναυλος βοὰ χαρᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 879 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 212}}; of oracles, ἀείδουσα.. βοὰς ἂς ἂν Ἀπόλλων κελαδήσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 92 (Lyric poetry)}}; **shout, murmur** of a crowd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 700c (_plural_); θόρυβος καὶ β. [prev. author] “Ti.” 70e}}; of things, **roar** of the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.48}}; **sound** of musical instruments, αὐλοὶ φόρμιγγές τε βοὴν ἔχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.495, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.8, “P.” 10.39 (_plural_); β. σάλπιγγος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 394}}; **cry** of birds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1021; θηρίων β. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1085}}; βοὴν θωΰσσειν, ἀϋτεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 335, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1092 (Lyric poetry); ἐφθέγξατο βοή τις [prev. author] “IT” 1386; βοάσομαι τὰν ὑπέρτονον βοάν 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 46 (Lyric poetry); βοὴν ἱστάναι 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 196.2}}; ὅσον καὶ ἀπὸ βοῆς ἕνεκα as far as **sound** went, only in appearance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.31.}}
**II** ={βοήθεια}, **aid called for, succour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 730, “Ag.” 1349. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1057 (Lyric poetry)}}. (**g[uglide]o[uglide]ā**, cf. Sanskrit _jō-guvē_ (intensive of **gávatē**) 'proclaim aloud'.)

996:
** βοήθ-εια**, ἡ
(_Doric dialect_ **βοάθοια** {{Refs|“SIG” 421.36 (from Thermon))}}, **help, aid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22, etc.; β. τῷ λόγῳ πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128c; ἡ ὑπὲρ τῶν δικαίων β. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.15}}; βοήθειαν ἔχειν πρὸς ὑγίειαν, πρὸς τὴν ἑκάστου ὑπερβολὴν μηχανᾶσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 651b1, 652a32}}: _nominative_ _singular_, as exclamation '**help!**', {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.8.5: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.302, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383a29; αἱ πρὸς εὔπλοιαν β. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.46, cf. NT.Act.27.17.}}
**2** **medical aid, cure**, κίνδυνος ἰσχυρότερος πάσης β. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 19.}}
**II** **force of auxiliaries**, ἡ παρὰ Διονυσίου β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.20; νεῶν β. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8: opposed to regular forces, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.32.}}

997:
** βοηθ-έω**
(sometimes written βοιηθέω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.237 (4th c.BC), “BGU” 1007.12 (3rd c.BC))}}, _Ionic dialect_ **βωθέω**, only {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus βωθέοντες, not in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (but compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 812.59) or 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}, compare βοηθήσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Michel” 12.15 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ **βοᾱθοέω** {{Refs|“SIG” 421.27 (from Thermon)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ βαθόημι (which see):—_middle_, _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 1.128}}:—**come to aid, succour, assist, aid**, with _dative_, τῇ σφετέρῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82; τοῖσιν ἠδικημένοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 79; πρὸς τοὺς αὑτῶν ψιλούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.3; τινὶ ἀντία τινός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99; τινὶ πρὸς τὸ ἄναντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.38}}; ναυσὶ β. τινί πολιορκουμένῳ{{Refs|[prev. work] 1.6.22; β. τοῖς φίλοις τὰ δίκαια [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.25}}; β. τοῖς τῶν προγόνων ἀτυχήμασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.169; β. τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88e}}; β. τῷ θεῷ **maintain** his **rights**, Epist. Philipp. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157; β. τοῖς νόμοις 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.33: with _dative_ et _accusative_, πατρὶ βοηθῶν θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 874c}}; of a physician, β. τῷ θερμῷ ἐπὶ τὸ ψυχρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13: _absolutely_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 19.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **come to the rescue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 7.158, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 613, etc.; β. παρά τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.57}}; ἐπί τινα **against** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.62, 4.125, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; β. ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103}}; ἐπὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.97, 4.72; ἐπὶ τὰς ναῦς [prev. author] 8.11; ἐκεῖσε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.41}}; β. πρός τι **contribute** to an object, see reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1155a14, compare “Metaphorical” 1079b16}}, or **keep** it **off**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Resp.” 474b24, “HA” 621a13}}; χρήμασι **with** money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1130a19: in Medicine texts, βοηθεῖ πρὸς τὸ κώνειον}} **it is an antidote** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.20.1; frequently in 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus as β. τοῖς φαγοῦσι 4.83.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be assisted, receive help**, παρά τινος {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383b28; βοηθήσομαι LXX.Dan.11.34, but βοηθηθήσομαι LXX.Isa.44.2}}; ἐβοήθην[{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 10.3, LXX.2Ch.26.15 (variant{ἐβοηθήθην}); ἵν᾽ ὦ βεβοηθημένη “PRyl.” 122.12 (2nd c.AD)}}]; especially of patients, **derive benefit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.82, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.687f: _impersonal_, ἐμοὶ βεβοήθηται τῷ τεθνεῶτι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.31; ταύτῃ μοι βεβοηθημένον ἐγεγόνει φιλοσοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 347e.}}

998:
Included with: ** βοηθ-όος**,
_Doric dialect_ **βοᾱ-**, ον, (βοή, θέω, compare βοη-δρόμος) **hasting to the cry for help** or **the call to arms**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.477}}; β. ἅρμα a chariot **hasting to the battle**, {{Refs|17.481.}}
**II** **aiding, helping**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 34}}:— _substantive_, **helper**, probably {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 12.103, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.23, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 27}}:—in Prose **βοηθός**, όν, **assisting, auxiliary**, νῆες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.45: with _dative_, ὁ τοῖς νόμοις β. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 53.1}}; frequently as _substantive_, **assistant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77, 6.100, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566b, +others; τῆς ἐπιτροπῆς “BGU” 1047iii 11 (2nd c.AD)}}; τοῦ στρατηγοῦ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1469.10 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}

999:
** βόθυν-ος**, ὁ,
={βόθρος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 210, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.3, “BGU” 1122.17 (1st c.BC).}}
**II** a meteorological phenomenon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342a36, “Mu.” 392b4.}}
